window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2011"] = {"id": "jammu-2011", "title": "University of Jammu 2011", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance 2011, booklet code TLV-17113 (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2011 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Individual's potential for growth and the role of unique perception in guiding behaviour and mental processes is emphasised in:", "options": {"a": "Psychodynamic Approach", "b": "Biological Approach", "c": "Behaviouristic Approach", "d": "Humanistic Approach"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the humanistic approach emphasises a person's potential for growth and his or her unique, subjective perception of the world as the guides of behaviour and mental processes. Humanistic psychology grew in the 1950s and 1960s as the 'third force', a reaction against psychoanalysis, which stressed unconscious conflict, and behaviourism, which stressed conditioning. Its two leading founders were Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1951). Maslow argued that once lower needs are met, people strive towards self-actualisation, becoming everything they are capable of being. Rogers argued that each person lives in a private phenomenal field, the world as he or she experiences it, and that behaviour follows this perceived world rather than the objective one. This focus on conscious personal experience is called phenomenology. Example: Meera and Arjun get the same marks in an exam. Meera sees the result as a sign that she can grow, so she joins a new course; Arjun sees it as failure and withdraws. A humanistic psychologist explains their different behaviour through their different perceptions and their drive towards growth. Option d is right because 'potential for growth' is self-actualisation and 'unique perception' is the phenomenal field, the two pillars of the humanistic approach. Exam trap: the psychodynamic approach also deals with inner mental life, so it tempts, but it stresses unconscious conflicts and early childhood, not conscious perception and growth; the behaviouristic approach is the one most opposed to the stem, because it leaves inner experience out altogether.", "topic": "Humanistic approach", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a researcher is using questionnaires to poll large groups of people, he is adopting:", "options": {"a": "Correlational method", "b": "Observational method", "c": "Clinical method", "d": "Survey method"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a researcher who uses questionnaires to poll large groups of people is using the survey method. A survey collects self-reports, through written questionnaires or structured interviews, from a large sample in order to describe the attitudes, opinions, beliefs or behaviour of a population. Fred N. Kerlinger (1910 to 1991) defined survey research as studying populations by selecting samples, to discover the incidence, distribution and interrelations of psychological and sociological variables. Its weaknesses are that people may give careless or socially desirable answers, the wording of questions can bias replies, and a sample that does not represent the population gives misleading results. Famous examples are the opinion polls of George Gallup (1901 to 1984), who correctly forecast the 1936 United States presidential election with a far smaller but better chosen sample than the Literary Digest's huge mail poll, and the Kinsey reports on sexual behaviour by Alfred Kinsey (1948, 1953). Example: Kavya wants to know how Indian college students feel about online classes, so she emails a 20-item questionnaire to 2,000 students across ten universities. Option d is right because questionnaires given to large groups are the defining tool of the survey method. Exam trap: survey data are often used to calculate correlations, so students pick 'correlational method'; but the stem describes how the data are collected, by asking many people, which is the survey, not the analysis of how two variables relate.", "topic": "Survey method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Curt Stern's \"Rubber Band\" analogy is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Heredity", "b": "Environment", "c": "Both Heredity & Environment", "d": "Behaviour"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Curt Stern's rubber band analogy shows that heredity and environment act together, so it is associated with both. Curt Stern (1902 to 1981) was a German-born American geneticist. He compared a person's genetic endowment for a trait such as height or intelligence to a rubber band. Heredity decides what kind of band you have: its unstretched length and how far it could possibly stretch. Environment decides how much it is actually stretched. A short band pulled hard by a rich environment can end up longer than a long band left slack in a poor one. So the visible trait (the phenotype) is never the product of genes alone or of environment alone, but of their interaction. The idea is close to the reaction range of Irving Gottesman (1963): genes set upper and lower limits, and the environment decides where within those limits a person lands. Example: Arjun and Kabir are born with similar genetic potential. Arjun gets good nutrition, books and coaching, while Kabir grows up malnourished with little schooling, so Arjun's measured intelligence is higher even though their 'bands' were alike. Option c is right because the whole purpose of the analogy is that genes fix the potential and the environment determines how much of it is realised. Exam trap: the band itself stands for the genes, so examiners hope you will answer 'heredity' alone. Key note: some guides key 'Heredity' because the band represents the genotype, but the analogy exists to show that heredity and environment jointly set the outcome, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Heredity and environment interaction", "fig": "Curt Stern"}, {"q": "Which form of child discipline tends to make children insecure, anxious and hungry for approval?", "options": {"a": "Authoritarian", "b": "Power Assertion", "c": "Withdrawal of Love", "d": "Authoritative"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: withdrawal of love is the form of discipline that tends to make children insecure, anxious and hungry for approval. Martin Hoffman, in work with Herbert Saltzstein (1967), sorted parental discipline into three techniques. Power assertion uses force, threats, physical punishment or removal of privileges. Love withdrawal shows anger or disappointment by withholding affection: ignoring the child, refusing to speak, turning away or threatening to leave. Induction explains how the child's act affects other people, and it best builds empathy, guilt and an inner conscience. Under love withdrawal the child often obeys, but because affection now seems conditional, he or she becomes anxious, insecure and eager to win approval back, instead of learning why the act was wrong. Power assertion, by contrast, is linked with hostility, aggression and weak moral internalisation. Example: when six-year-old Riya spills juice, her mother stops talking to her for the whole evening. Riya follows her mother around, apologising and asking 'Are you still angry?', the anxious, approval-hungry pattern of love withdrawal. Option c is right because the stem's three outcomes, insecurity, anxiety and craving for approval, are the textbook effects of withholding affection. Exam trap: 'authoritarian' and 'authoritative' look like discipline answers, but they are Diana Baumrind's (1966) parenting styles, broad climates of warmth and control, not specific discipline techniques. Key note: Baumrind's authoritarian children are also described as anxious and withdrawn, but 'hungry for approval' is the classic marker of love withdrawal and the stem asks for a form of discipline, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Love withdrawal discipline", "fig": "Martin Hoffman"}, {"q": "Learning that is not demonstrated at the time it occurs is:", "options": {"a": "Insight learning", "b": "Social learning", "c": "Observational learning", "d": "Latent learning"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: learning that is not demonstrated at the time it occurs is latent learning. Latent means hidden. In latent learning a person or animal learns something without any reward, but the learning stays out of sight until there is a reason to use it. The classic evidence comes from Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930). Three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze every day. One group always found food in the goal box, one never did, and the third found no food for the first ten days and then found food from day 11. Once food appeared, the third group's errors dropped almost at once to the level of the group rewarded all along, and soon even below it. These rats must have learned the maze during the unrewarded days, forming what Tolman called a cognitive map, and simply had no reason to show it. The study challenged the strict behaviourist view that reinforcement is needed for learning: reinforcement affects performance, not learning itself. Example: Kabir rides as a passenger to college every day and never drives. One day his friend falls ill, and Kabir drives the route without a single wrong turn; he had learned it all along. Option d is right because the defining feature of latent learning is the gap between learning and its display. Exam trap: observational learning is also often shown later, which tempts students, but the term for learning that stays hidden until an incentive appears is latent learning.", "topic": "Latent learning", "fig": "Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik"}, {"q": "When a child learns to fear the doctor's clinic by associating it with the reflexive emotional reaction to a painful injection, the process is known as:", "options": {"a": "Stimulus generalisation", "b": "Stimulus discrimination", "c": "Acquisition", "d": "Extinction"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: when a child learns to fear the clinic because it has been paired with a painful injection, the process is acquisition, the first stage of classical conditioning. Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed that when a neutral stimulus is paired repeatedly with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) that naturally triggers a reflex, the neutral stimulus alone comes to trigger a similar response. Here the injection is the UCS, the reflexive pain and fear is the unconditioned response (UCR), the clinic becomes the conditioned stimulus (CS), and fear of the clinic is the conditioned response (CR). Acquisition is the period in which this CS and UCS link is formed and the CR grows stronger with each pairing, fastest when the CS comes just before the UCS. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) used the same process to condition fear in the infant Little Albert, pairing a white rat with a loud noise. Example: after two painful vaccinations, three-year-old Ishaan starts crying as soon as the car turns into the clinic's car park, before any needle is in sight. Option c is right because the stem describes the fear being learned for the first time through pairing. Exam trap: generalisation, discrimination and extinction all act on a CR that already exists; the stem says nothing about similar stimuli, about telling stimuli apart or about the fear fading.", "topic": "Acquisition in classical conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Chunking technique is used to enhance the capacity of:", "options": {"a": "Sensory memory", "b": "Short-term memory", "c": "Long-term memory", "d": "Episodic memory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: chunking is used to increase the capacity of short-term memory. Short-term memory (STM) holds a small amount of information for about 15 to 30 seconds unless it is rehearsed. George Miller (1956), in his paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', showed that its span is about 7 plus or minus 2 units, whether the units are digits, letters or words. The crucial point is that the limit is set in units, not in raw pieces of information. Chunking means grouping separate items into larger meaningful units, so that each chunk fills only one slot. The more a person knows, the bigger the chunks can be: a chess master sees a whole board as a few familiar patterns. Example: Riya must remember the digits 9 8 4 5 6 3 2 1 0 7. Ten separate digits exceed most people's span, but grouped like a mobile number, 98456 32107, they become two chunks. Likewise the letters I N D I A I I T I S R O become three chunks: INDIA, IIT, ISRO. Option b is right because chunking works around the small number of slots, the defining bottleneck of short-term memory. Exam trap: chunking also helps later storage, so 'long-term memory' tempts, but long-term memory has no known capacity limit to enlarge; the capacity problem chunking solves belongs to short-term memory.", "topic": "Chunking and short-term memory", "fig": "George Miller"}, {"q": "Achievement and recognition are ______ needs whereas affiliation and acceptance are ______ needs:", "options": {"a": "Physiological, Safety", "b": "Safety, Belongingness", "c": "Self-actualisation, esteem", "d": "Esteem, Belongingness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: achievement and recognition are esteem needs, whereas affiliation and acceptance are belongingness needs. Abraham Maslow (1943) arranged human needs in a hierarchy of five levels. From the bottom they are: physiological needs (food, water, sleep, warmth); safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear); belongingness and love needs (affiliation, friendship, acceptance, being part of a group); esteem needs (achievement, competence, mastery, recognition, status, respect from others and self-respect); and self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. Lower needs are more pressing, or prepotent: a starving person thinks of food, not fame. The first four levels are deficiency needs, felt when something is missing, and self-actualisation is a growth need. Example: Arjun has just moved to Pune for a job. First he wants to make friends and be accepted by his colleagues (belongingness). Once he feels part of the team, he works for a promotion and the 'employee of the month' award (esteem). Option d is right because achievement and recognition concern competence and respect, the esteem level, while affiliation and acceptance concern being loved and included, the belongingness level just below it. Exam trap: option c shifts each label one level too high, putting self-actualisation where esteem belongs and esteem where belongingness belongs.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "\"I am afraid because I am aroused\" explains:", "options": {"a": "James-Lange theory", "b": "Cannon-Bard theory", "c": "Cognitive arousal theory", "d": "Common sense theory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'I am afraid because I am aroused' expresses the James-Lange theory of emotion. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885), working independently, reversed the common sense order of emotion. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. James argued that we see the bear, tremble and run, and the feeling of fear is our awareness of those bodily changes: we are afraid because we tremble. So the stimulus produces bodily arousal first, and the felt emotion is the perception of that arousal. This implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily change. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory, arguing that the internal organs react too slowly and too similarly across emotions to produce distinct feelings. Example: Meera is walking home at night and hears footsteps behind her. Her heart pounds and her legs shake; on the James-Lange view, sensing the pounding and shaking is what makes her feel afraid. Option a is right because the stem puts arousal first and treats fear as the result of that arousal. Exam trap: the two-factor theory also contains arousal, so 'cognitive arousal theory' tempts; but there arousal alone is not enough, and fear needs a cognitive label for the arousal, whereas the stem has no label, only 'because I am aroused'.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "Both mental age and chronological age of Aisha is 12. What will be her IQ?", "options": {"a": "120", "b": "12", "c": "100", "d": "Between 100 and 120"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: when Aisha's mental age and chronological age are both 12, her IQ is 100. The ratio IQ compares mental age (MA), the age level of the test items a child can pass, with chronological age (CA), her actual age. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing MA by CA, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the result by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to remove decimals: IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. For Aisha: (12 / 12) x 100 = 1 x 100 = 100. An IQ of 100 means the child performs exactly at the level typical of her age, neither ahead nor behind. If MA is above CA, the IQ is above 100 (MA 15 at CA 12 gives 125); if MA is below CA, it is below 100 (MA 9 at CA 12 gives 75). The ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops rising in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, which has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. On that scale too, average performance for one's age is 100. Option c is right because equal mental and chronological ages give a ratio of 1, and 1 x 100 is 100. Exam trap: '12' tempts because both ages are 12, but 12 is the mental age or the chronological age, not the quotient; the IQ is the ratio multiplied by 100.", "topic": "Ratio IQ calculation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Thinking that produces many ideas or alternatives is known as:", "options": {"a": "Divergent thinking", "b": "Convergent thinking", "c": "Critical thinking", "d": "Creative thinking"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: thinking that produces many ideas or alternatives is divergent thinking. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, argued that intelligence tests ignored creativity because they rewarded only one kind of thinking. He separated convergent thinking, which narrows information down to the single correct answer, from divergent thinking, which branches out from one starting point to many possible answers; in his Structure of Intellect model he called it divergent production. Divergent thinking is scored on fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (number of different categories), originality (how unusual the ideas are) and elaboration (how much detail is added). The classic task is Alternate Uses: 'list all the uses of a brick'. Ellis Paul Torrance later built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) on the same idea. Example: asked for uses of a newspaper, Kavya lists reading, wrapping, a fan, a paper boat, a floor mat and a mask for a school play; her long, varied list shows divergent thinking. Option a is right because 'producing many ideas or alternatives' is the exact definition of divergent thinking. Exam trap: 'creative thinking' sounds right, but creativity is the broader outcome and also needs ideas to be judged and refined. Key note: divergent thinking is the core of creativity, so 'creative thinking' is close, but the specific term for producing many ideas or alternatives is divergent thinking, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Divergent and convergent thinking", "fig": "Joy Paul Guilford"}, {"q": "Guilford's SOI model contains:", "options": {"a": "4 contents × 6 products × 5 operations", "b": "4 contents × 5 products × 6 operations", "c": "5 contents × 4 products × 6 operations", "d": "6 contents × 5 products × 4 operations"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Guilford's Structure of Intellect (SOI) model contains 4 contents x 6 products x 5 operations, giving 120 abilities. Joy Paul Guilford (1967), in The Nature of Human Intelligence, rejected a single general intelligence and pictured intellect as a cube with three faces. Each ability is one cell of the cube, defined by one choice from each face. Operations are what the mind does: cognition, memory, divergent production, convergent production and evaluation (5). Contents are what it works on: figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural (4). Products are the form the result takes: units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications (6). So 4 x 6 x 5 = 120 separate abilities. A memory aid is COP, 4 5 6: Contents 4, Operations 5, Products 6. Example: when Rohan spots the odd word out in 'mango, apple, potato, banana', he is using cognition (operation) of semantic (content) classes (product), one cell of the cube. Option a is right because it is the only option that gives the correct number for every face of the 1967 model. Exam trap: option b keeps the total of 120 but swaps two numbers, giving 5 products and 6 operations, so check each face, not just the product. Key note: Guilford later split figural content into visual and auditory (150 abilities) and memory into memory recording and memory retention (180 abilities); none of the options matches those later versions, so the classic 1967 model and the answer used here stand.", "topic": "Guilford's Structure of Intellect", "fig": "Joy Paul Guilford"}, {"q": "A social learning process whereby individuals come to internalize or accept their own culture or subculture patterns is termed as:", "options": {"a": "Group", "b": "Society", "c": "Socialization", "d": "Social facilitation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the social learning process by which individuals come to internalise and accept the patterns of their own culture or subculture is socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong process through which a person learns the norms, values, beliefs, roles, language and customs of his or her society, and comes to hold them as his or her own rather than merely obeying them. It turns a newborn into a functioning member of a group. The family is the chief agent in early childhood (primary socialisation); school, peers, religion, the workplace and the mass media follow (secondary socialisation). It works through instruction, reward and punishment, and imitation of models. Adopting a group's norms before joining it is anticipatory socialisation, a term of Robert Merton (1910 to 2003); unlearning old norms and learning new ones after a major change, such as joining the army, is resocialisation. Example: Ananya grows up in a Tamil family in Chennai. Without formal lessons she learns to touch elders' feet, to eat with her right hand and to celebrate Pongal, and as an adult she follows these customs because they feel right to her, not because anyone tells her to. Option c is right because the stem's key words, learning and internalising one's culture's patterns, define socialisation. Exam trap: 'social facilitation' also begins with 'social' and sounds like a learning process, but it concerns performance in front of others, not absorbing a culture.", "topic": "Socialisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most suitable method for understanding the social taboos is:", "options": {"a": "Case study", "b": "Interview", "c": "Observation", "d": "Experimental"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: observation is the most suitable method for understanding social taboos. A taboo is a strong, usually unwritten prohibition backed by a sense of the sacred, disgust or shame: rules about what may not be eaten, touched, said or done, and with whom. Taboos are hard to study by asking. People are reluctant to talk about forbidden subjects, often cannot put unspoken rules into words, and tend to give polite or socially desirable answers. What taboos do leave is a trace in behaviour: what people avoid, how they react when a rule is broken, which places and objects are kept apart. So researchers watch real behaviour in natural settings, usually through participant observation, in which the observer lives with the group. This is the classic method of anthropology, made famous by the fieldwork of Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) in the Trobriand Islands. Example: Sneha wants to understand menstrual taboos in a village. Women give short, guarded answers in interviews, but by living there for months she sees who enters the kitchen and temple, who sleeps apart and how others respond. Option c is right because observation records what people actually do and avoid, without relying on what they are willing to say. Exam trap: the interview looks like the natural way to learn about beliefs, but taboo subjects are exactly the ones people avoid discussing. Key note: interviews can add meaning once trust exists, but observation of real behaviour is the method texts single out for taboos, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Methods for studying taboos", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Charismatic leaders are also known as:", "options": {"a": "Autocratic leaders", "b": "Democratic leaders", "c": "Transformational leaders", "d": "Unique leaders"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: charismatic leaders are also known as transformational leaders. The sociologist Max Weber (1922) described charismatic authority, power that rests on followers' devotion to a leader seen as exceptional, unlike traditional authority (a king) or legal-rational authority (an official). Robert House (1977) turned this into a theory of charismatic leadership: such leaders voice a vision, show strong confidence and conviction, model the behaviour they want and set high expectations, and followers trust and identify with them. James MacGregor Burns (1978) contrasted transforming leaders, who raise followers' motives and values, with transactional leaders, who exchange rewards for effort. Bernard Bass (1985) developed transformational leadership further, and it is now usually described by four components: idealised influence (charisma), inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation and individualised consideration. Because charisma is its central component, textbooks treat the two labels as near synonyms. Example: at a struggling Bengaluru start-up, the founder Nikhil shares a vivid vision of affordable health care for villages; his team works late not for bonuses but because they believe in him and the mission. Option c is right because charisma is the heart of transformational leadership: both describe leaders who inspire followers to go beyond self-interest. Exam trap: 'autocratic' and 'democratic' are familiar leadership labels too, but they come from a different scheme, styles of making decisions, and say nothing about inspiration or vision.", "topic": "Charismatic and transformational leadership", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In communication process radio, T.V., mobile phones are all examples of:", "options": {"a": "Receivers", "b": "Senders", "c": "Feedback", "d": "Channels"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: radio, television and mobile phones are all channels in the communication process. Communication is the transfer of a message from one person to another, and the classic models break it into parts. Claude Shannon and Warren Weaver (1949) described a source, a transmitter, a channel, noise, a receiver and a destination. David Berlo (1960) simplified this into the SMCR model: Source, Message, Channel, Receiver. The source, or sender, has an idea and encodes it into a message of words, pictures or signals. The channel is the medium or pathway through which the message travels: face to face speech, a letter, a telephone line, radio, television, the internet. The receiver decodes the message, and feedback is the receiver's response sent back to the source, which turns a one-way process into a two-way one. Example: Pooja's college announces exam dates on the notice board, in a WhatsApp group and on local radio. The exam office is the sender, the dates are the message, Pooja and her classmates are the receivers, and the notice board, WhatsApp and radio are the channels. Option d is right because radio, TV and phones are not people who send or receive, but media that carry the message between them. Exam trap: because a phone or TV set 'receives' a signal, students pick 'receivers'; in the model the receiver is the person who decodes the message, and the device is the channel.", "topic": "Channels in communication", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following represents the cognitive component of an attitude?", "options": {"a": "I just love Indian food", "b": "Tonight we are going to that new Indian restaurant", "c": "Indian food is the best of the Asian foods", "d": "All the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: 'Indian food is the best of the Asian foods' represents the cognitive component of an attitude. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of a person, object, group or idea. Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) proposed the tricomponent, or ABC, model, in which every attitude has three parts. The affective component is the feeling or emotion attached to the object, such as liking, love, fear or disgust. The behavioural (conative) component is the action, or intention to act, towards it. The cognitive component is the set of beliefs, thoughts and judgements one holds about it: statements of what one thinks is true of the object. A quick test is to ask what a sentence expresses: a feeling ('I love'), a plan or action ('we are going') or a claim about the object ('it is the best'). Example: Rahul's attitude to cricket has all three parts: 'cricket is the most strategic sport' (cognitive), 'I get excited when India bats' (affective) and 'I have booked tickets for the Test match' (behavioural). Option c is right because 'Indian food is the best of the Asian foods' is a belief or judgement about the food, not a feeling or an action. Exam trap: option a sounds like an opinion, but 'I just love' expresses emotion, so it is the affective component; the cognitive component is the belief about the object's qualities.", "topic": "ABC model of attitudes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Treating members of various social groups differently in circumstances where their rights or treatments should be identical is:", "options": {"a": "Prejudice", "b": "Racism", "c": "Discrimination", "d": "Dogmatism"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: treating members of different social groups differently in situations where their rights or treatment should be identical is discrimination. Social psychologists separate three linked ideas about groups. A stereotype is a belief about the typical traits of a group's members, the cognitive part. Prejudice is a negative attitude or feeling towards people because of their group membership, mainly the affective part; Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, called it an antipathy based on a faulty and inflexible generalisation. Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair, negative or unequal action towards people because of their group, such as refusing them jobs, housing or service. Prejudice is in the mind; discrimination is in what people do. The two can come apart: a person may hold a prejudice but not act on it because of laws or norms, or may discriminate by following an unfair rule without feeling hostile. Example: Rakesh and Imran have identical degrees and interview scores, but a landlord rents the flat to Rakesh after learning that Imran belongs to a different community. Whatever the landlord feels, the unequal treatment is discrimination. Option c is right because the stem describes behaviour, treating people differently, where equal treatment is due. Exam trap: prejudice and discrimination are often used loosely as synonyms, but prejudice is the attitude and discrimination is the action; the stem's word 'treating' points to the action.", "topic": "Prejudice versus discrimination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The general reluctance among people in transmitting bad news to others is called:", "options": {"a": "Interpersonal Attraction", "b": "MUM effect", "c": "Propinquity", "d": "Affiliation effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the general reluctance of people to pass bad news on to others is called the MUM effect. Sidney Rosen and Abraham Tesser (1970) named it from the phrase 'keep Mum about Undesirable Messages'. In their studies, people who had to relay news to someone else passed on good news readily but delayed, softened or avoided bad news, even when they had played no part in causing it. Several reasons have been offered: the bearer does not want to feel guilty or share in the receiver's distress, fears being linked with the bad news and disliked (the 'shoot the messenger' problem), and follows a social norm against hurting others. The effect matters in workplaces and hospitals: employees hide project problems from their bosses, and doctors may delay telling a patient about a poor diagnosis. Example: Vikram's team learns that a major client has cancelled its contract. Each member waits for someone else to tell the manager, so she hears about it two days late. Option b is right because the MUM effect is the specific name for reluctance to transmit undesirable messages. Exam trap: the other options are real or plausible social psychology terms about relations between people, but none concerns passing on bad news.", "topic": "MUM effect", "fig": "Sidney Rosen and Abraham Tesser"}, {"q": "The most contributing gas to global warming and the greenhouse effect is:", "options": {"a": "Carbon dioxide", "b": "Methane", "c": "Chlorofluorocarbons", "d": "Nitrogen"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: carbon dioxide contributes most to global warming and the human-enhanced greenhouse effect. The greenhouse effect is the warming of Earth's surface by gases that let incoming sunlight through but absorb and re-emit the heat (infrared radiation) that the Earth gives off, much as the glass of a greenhouse keeps heat in. A natural greenhouse effect keeps the planet habitable; global warming is the extra warming since the industrial revolution caused by human emissions. Carbon dioxide (CO2) comes mainly from burning coal, oil and gas and from cutting down forests. Molecule for molecule it traps less heat than methane or CFCs, but it is emitted in vastly larger amounts and much of it stays in the air for centuries, so it accounts for about two thirds of the extra heating from long-lived greenhouse gases. Environmental psychology studies the human side: why people keep high-carbon habits and how to change them. Example: Aditya works out that his daily 30 km commute by petrol car emits several kilograms of CO2; switching to the metro cuts this sharply, the kind of behaviour change environmental psychologists try to encourage. Option a is right because CO2's sheer volume and long lifetime give it the largest total warming effect of all human-emitted gases. Exam trap: methane is far more potent per molecule, so students pick b, but potency is not total contribution; water vapour, the largest natural greenhouse gas, acts as a feedback and is not among the options.", "topic": "Greenhouse gases and global warming", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Carl Jung is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Individual Psychology", "b": "Analytical Psychology", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Personal Psychology"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Carl Jung is associated with analytical psychology. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), a Swiss psychiatrist, was once the favourite and chosen successor of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), but the two broke in 1913, mainly because Jung rejected Freud's view that libido is chiefly sexual energy. Jung then called his own system analytical psychology. He divided the psyche into three layers: the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten or repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious, a deeper layer inherited by all humans. The collective unconscious holds archetypes, universal patterns such as the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, denied side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in men and the masculine side in women) and the self (the archetype of wholeness). Jung also gave psychology the attitudes of introversion and extraversion and four functions: thinking, feeling, sensing and intuiting. Example: Meera dreams of a wise old woman guiding her through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read the figure as an archetype from the collective unconscious, not only as a disguised personal wish. Option b is right because analytical psychology is the name Jung gave to his school after leaving psychoanalysis. Exam trap: the neo-Freudian school names are easily swapped: individual psychology belongs to Alfred Adler (1912), psychoanalysis to Freud, and the word 'personal' appears only in Jung's personal unconscious, which is one layer of his model, not a school.", "topic": "Jung's analytical psychology", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Over secretion from thyroid glands may lead to:", "options": {"a": "Dull personality", "b": "Nervous personality", "c": "Disobedience", "d": "Fattiness"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: over-secretion of the thyroid gland tends to produce a nervous personality. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the neck. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones that set the body's metabolic rate, the speed at which cells burn fuel for energy. Too much thyroxine (hyperthyroidism, most often caused by Graves' disease, an autoimmune condition) speeds everything up: the person becomes restless, tense, irritable, anxious and excitable, sleeps poorly, has a fast heartbeat and trembling hands, feels hot and loses weight despite eating well. Too little thyroxine (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: sluggishness, poor concentration, low mood, feeling cold and weight gain in adults, and cretinism (stunted physical and mental growth) in infants if it is not treated early. Example: Kabir, a college student, becomes jittery, snaps at friends and loses six kilos in two months; a blood test shows an overactive thyroid, and his nervousness settles once treatment brings his hormone level down. This is why clinicians check thyroid function before diagnosing an anxiety disorder. Option b is right because excess thyroxine overdrives metabolism and arousal, giving the nervous, overactive picture. Exam trap: dullness and weight gain are signs of too little thyroxine, the reverse of the stem, so under-secretion signs are placed beside the correct over-secretion sign to catch students who remember only that the thyroid affects personality.", "topic": "Thyroid over-secretion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Personality is a dynamic organisation within an individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustment to his environment.\" This definition of personality was given by:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Cattell", "c": "Adler", "d": "Allport"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: this definition of personality was given by Gordon Allport. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, reviewed about 50 earlier definitions and proposed his own: personality is \"the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment\". Each phrase carries an idea. Dynamic organisation means personality keeps growing and changing, yet stays organised rather than being a random heap of habits. Psychophysical systems means it involves both mind and body: traits, attitudes and habits, but also nerves and glands. Determine means personality actively drives behaviour rather than just describing it. Unique adjustments stresses that no two people are alike, which fits Allport's idiographic approach, the in-depth study of the single individual. In 1961 he replaced the last phrase with \"characteristic behavior and thought\". Example: Riya and Arjun face the same job interview; Riya calms herself by rehearsing notes and Arjun by joking with the receptionist. Allport would say each shows a unique adjustment produced by a different organisation of traits. Option d is right because the wording 'dynamic organisation, psychophysical systems, unique adjustment' is Allport's classic 1937 definition, the most quoted in the field. Exam trap: Raymond Cattell (1950) also gave a famous definition, personality is \"that which permits a prediction of what a person will do in a given situation\", so students who recall only 'a trait theorist's definition' may pick Cattell.", "topic": "Allport's definition of personality", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "According to Psychoanalytic theory of personality, Oedipus and Electra conflicts develop during:", "options": {"a": "Anal Stage", "b": "Phallic Stage", "c": "Latency Stage", "d": "Genital Stage"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Oedipus and Electra conflicts develop during the phallic stage. Sigmund Freud (1905) set out his theory of infantile sexuality in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality and completed it over the next two decades: the libido (sexual energy) centres on a different body zone at each age, giving five psychosexual stages: oral (birth to about 1, the mouth), anal (about 1 to 3, toilet training), phallic (about 3 to 6, the genitals), latency (about 6 to puberty, when sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school and friendships) and genital (puberty onward, mature sexuality). In the phallic stage the child becomes aware of the genitals, desires the opposite-sex parent and feels rivalry with the same-sex parent. In boys this is the Oedipus complex, with castration anxiety. For girls Carl Jung (1913) named the parallel the Electra complex, linked by Freud to penis envy, though Freud himself disliked the term. The conflict is resolved by identification with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego and the child's gender role. Example: four-year-old Kabir announces he will marry his mother and sulks when his father hugs her; by six he copies his father's walk and talk, a sign of identification. Option b is right because these conflicts are the defining crisis of the phallic stage. Exam trap: the genital stage sounds the most sexual and is often chosen, but it begins at puberty, when the early conflicts revive rather than first appear.", "topic": "Phallic stage, Oedipus complex", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "According to earlier views, psychological disorders stem from:", "options": {"a": "Natural causes", "b": "Biological causes", "c": "Supernatural causes", "d": "Psychological causes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to the earliest views, psychological disorders stem from supernatural causes. This oldest explanation, called demonology or the supernatural model, blamed strange behaviour on possession by evil spirits, the anger of gods, sin or witchcraft. Treatment followed the supposed cause. Some Stone Age skulls show trephining, a hole chipped through the skull, perhaps to let an evil spirit escape. Later societies used prayer, magic and exorcism, and in late medieval Europe witch hunts were guided by the Malleus Maleficarum (1487), a manual for identifying witches. The Greek physician Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) made the first break: he argued that mental illness had natural causes in the brain and body. His humoral theory, later developed by the Greek physician Galen (second century AD), who worked in Rome, linked melancholia to an excess of black bile. Psychological causes were stressed much later, from Franz Anton Mesmer in the 1770s and the hypnotists of the Nancy school to Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895, Studies on Hysteria). Example: when Meera's grandmother in a village begins hearing voices, the family calls an exorcist instead of a doctor; that is the supernatural view still at work. Option c is right because demonology, the first known explanation of abnormality, placed the cause outside nature, in spirits and magic. Exam trap: 'natural' and 'biological' causes sound ancient because Hippocrates was ancient, but his naturalistic view replaced the even older supernatural one.", "topic": "Demonology, supernatural model", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to DSM-IV classification, Axis-I contains:", "options": {"a": "Personality disorders", "b": "General medical conditions", "c": "Clinical disorders", "d": "Psychosocial and environmental problems"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: in DSM-IV, Axis I contains the clinical disorders. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, published by the American Psychiatric Association, used a multiaxial system from DSM-III (1980) through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000). Each patient was described on five axes so that the clinician would look beyond the main diagnosis to the whole person. Axis I: clinical disorders, such as major depression, schizophrenia, phobias, eating disorders and substance use disorders, plus other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation (now called intellectual disability). Axis III: general medical conditions relevant to the case, such as diabetes or hypothyroidism. Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems, such as job loss, divorce or poverty. Axis V: the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a 0 to 100 rating of overall functioning. Example: Arjun's case file might read Axis I major depressive disorder; Axis II none; Axis III hypertension; Axis IV recent job loss; Axis V GAF 55. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes and merged the first three into a single list of diagnoses. Option c is right because Axis I was reserved for the clinical disorders that usually bring a person for treatment. Exam trap: the other options are the remaining axes in scrambled order, and students who remember only that personality disorders sat on a 'separate axis' may put them on Axis I instead of Axis II.", "topic": "DSM-IV multiaxial system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The unpleasant and unwanted thought that keeps coming up to mind on and off in spite of a person's efforts to resist refers to:", "options": {"a": "Phobia", "b": "PTSD", "c": "Obsession", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive Disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: an unpleasant, unwanted thought that keeps returning despite the person's efforts to resist it is an obsession. In DSM-IV and DSM-5, obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, urges or images that are felt as intrusive and unwanted and that cause marked anxiety or distress; the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Common themes are contamination, doubt ('did I lock the door?'), harm, symmetry and forbidden sexual or religious thoughts. Unlike delusions, obsessions are usually recognised as products of one's own mind. Compulsions are the repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) carried out to reduce the anxiety the obsession creates. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is the diagnosis given when obsessions, compulsions or both take up more than an hour a day or cause marked distress or impairment. Example: Meera keeps picturing germs on her hands that will make her family ill; she knows the idea is excessive but cannot shake it. That intrusive thought is the obsession; washing her hands twenty times is the compulsion; the whole pattern, if it disrupts her life, is OCD. Option c is right because the stem describes one kind of symptom, the intrusive thought itself, which is the definition of an obsession. Exam trap: OCD is tempting because it contains the word 'obsessive', but OCD names the whole disorder, while the stem asks only for the thought.", "topic": "Obsessions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Raj is a fourteen year old boy and has committed theft. He will be considered as:", "options": {"a": "Sociopath", "b": "Psychopath", "c": "Delinquent", "d": "Criminal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: a fourteen-year-old boy who commits theft is considered a delinquent (a juvenile delinquent). Juvenile delinquency means an act by a minor that would be a crime if an adult did it, such as theft, assault or vandalism. The law treats young offenders separately because they are still developing and seen as open to reform. In India, the Juvenile Justice Act of 1986 set the limit at under 16 for boys and under 18 for girls; the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2000 made it under 18 for everyone, and the 2015 Act calls such a person a 'child in conflict with law', whose case goes to a Juvenile Justice Board rather than an ordinary criminal court. The aim is rehabilitation (counselling, observation homes, probation), not punishment. In clinical terms, a repeated pattern of serious rule breaking in a child may be diagnosed as conduct disorder, but delinquency itself is a legal and social label. Example: Raj's case would be heard by the Juvenile Justice Board, which might place him under the supervision of a probation officer while he continues school. Option c is right because Raj is a minor, so his theft is handled as delinquency. Exam trap: 'psychopath' and 'sociopath' sound like explanations for antisocial acts, but they are personality labels for a lasting callous pattern, and antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before 18; a single theft at 14 makes Raj a delinquent, not a psychopath.", "topic": "Juvenile delinquency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who is considered as father of experimental psychology?", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "J. Watson", "c": "William James", "d": "William Wundt"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology (the option spells his first name the English way, William). Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician and physiologist, argued that conscious experience could be studied with the controlled methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) mapped out the new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig he and his students began formal experiments in what is taken as the first psychology laboratory. For this reason 1879 is treated as the birth year of psychology as an independent science. He also founded the first journal for experimental psychology, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained many of the first generation of psychologists from Europe and America. His method was experimental introspection: trained observers responded to precisely controlled stimuli (clicks, flashes of light, metronome beats) and reported their immediate experience while their reaction times were measured. Example: in a Wundt-style practical, Kabir presses a key the instant he hears a click, then again only after he has clearly recognised the sound; the extra time estimates how long recognition takes. Option d is right because Wundt gave psychology its first formal laboratory, its first experimental journal and its first trained experimenters. Exam trap: each other name is the 'father' of something else: Sigmund Freud (1900) of psychoanalysis, John B. Watson (1913) of behaviourism and William James (1890) of American psychology; Gustav Fechner (1860) is sometimes also credited, as the founder of psychophysics.", "topic": "Father of experimental psychology", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "In a controlled situation, the group of subjects was exposed to all experimental conditions or variables except to the independent variable. This group of subjects is:", "options": {"a": "Independent group", "b": "Dependent group", "c": "Control group", "d": "Extraneous group"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the group exposed to every condition of the experiment except the independent variable is the control group. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable (IV), the presumed cause, and measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV), the outcome. Participants who receive the IV (the treatment) form the experimental group. Participants who are treated in exactly the same way, with the same room, instructions, timing and measurements, but who do not receive the IV, form the control group. Because the two groups differ only in the IV, any difference in the DV can be put down to it. The control group therefore provides a baseline: it shows what would have happened anyway. Random assignment, in which chance decides who goes into each group, makes the groups equal at the start. In drug trials the control group often receives a placebo, an inactive pill, so that expectations are equal as well. Example: Riya tests whether background music improves memory; one group of students learns a word list with music playing (experimental group) and another learns the same list in the same room in silence (control group). Option c is right because the stem describes exactly the group that receives everything except the IV. Exam trap: the other options borrow words from the language of variables ('independent', 'dependent', 'extraneous') and turn them into made-up group names, which misleads students who half remember the vocabulary.", "topic": "Control group", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The minimum amount of energy that can be detected 50 percent of the time is:", "options": {"a": "Absolute threshold", "b": "Difference threshold", "c": "Response criterion", "d": "Signal detection"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the minimum amount of stimulus energy that can be detected 50 per cent of the time is the absolute threshold. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, founded psychophysics, the measurement of the link between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. He found that there is no sharp line between 'not sensed' and 'sensed': as a stimulus grows stronger, the chance of detecting it rises gradually. So the absolute threshold is defined statistically, as the intensity detected on half of the trials. Classic everyday estimates (Eugene Galanter, 1962) are striking: on a clear, dark night a candle flame can be seen about 30 miles away, and a watch ticking can be heard about 20 feet away in a quiet room. Fechner's methods for finding thresholds are the method of limits, the method of constant stimuli and the method of adjustment (average error). Example: Arjun sits in a dark room while a dim light is flashed many times at different strengths; the strength he reports seeing on half the trials is his absolute threshold for light. Option a is right because 'minimum energy detected 50 per cent of the time' is the textbook definition of the absolute threshold. Exam trap: the difference threshold (just noticeable difference) uses the same 50 per cent rule, but it is the smallest change between two stimuli that can be noticed, not the weakest stimulus that can be detected at all.", "topic": "Absolute threshold", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Variable that might have affected the dependent variable along with or instead of the independent variable is known as:", "options": {"a": "Dependent variable", "b": "Confounding variable", "c": "Control variable", "d": "Independent variable"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a variable that may have affected the dependent variable along with, or instead of, the independent variable is a confounding variable. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV). Any other variable that could influence the DV is an extraneous variable. An extraneous variable becomes a confounding variable when it changes systematically together with the IV, so its effect is mixed up (confounded) with the IV's effect. The researcher then cannot tell whether the IV, the confound or both produced the result, and the study loses internal validity, its ability to show cause and effect. Common confounds include time of day, experimenter expectations, pre-existing differences between groups and order effects. They are controlled by random assignment, holding conditions constant, counterbalancing and blinding. Example: Arjun tests a new teaching method on a morning class and the old method on an afternoon class. The morning class scores higher, but those students may simply be fresher, so time of day is confounded with teaching method and the result proves nothing. Option b is right because the stem describes a variable whose effect cannot be separated from, and may even replace, the effect of the IV. Exam trap: 'control variable' sounds close, but a control variable is one the researcher deliberately holds constant precisely so that it cannot become a confound.", "topic": "Confounding variable", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a person perceives an incomplete triangle as a complete one, he is following ______ principle of perception.", "options": {"a": "Closure", "b": "Contrast", "c": "Figure and Ground", "d": "Similarity and Continuity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: perceiving an incomplete triangle as a complete one follows the principle of closure. The Gestalt psychologists, Max Wertheimer (1923), Kurt Koffka (1935) and Wolfgang Kohler (1929), argued that perception is organised into meaningful wholes and that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. Wertheimer described laws of perceptual organisation by which the brain groups separate elements. Closure is the tendency to fill in gaps and see an incomplete figure as complete and closed. The other grouping laws include proximity (nearby items are grouped), similarity (items that look alike are grouped) and continuity (we follow smooth, continuous lines and curves). Figure-ground organisation, seeing an object stand out from its background, is even more basic. All of these serve the broader law of Pragnanz, the tendency to see the simplest, most stable form available. Example: Riya glances at a logo made of three black corners with gaps between them, yet she immediately sees a triangle; her perception has closed the gaps. Closure is also why she can read a word on the blackboard that is partly rubbed out. Option a is right because filling in the missing sides to see a whole triangle is the definition of closure. Exam trap: continuity is tempting because the eye seems to 'continue' the lines across the gaps, but continuity is about following an unbroken line or curve, while closure is about completing a figure into a whole.", "topic": "Gestalt closure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Linear perspective and interposition are examples of:", "options": {"a": "Binocular cues", "b": "Monocular cues", "c": "Binocular Disparity", "d": "Convergence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: linear perspective and interposition are examples of monocular cues. Depth cues are pieces of information that let us judge distance and see a three-dimensional world from the flat images on our retinas. Monocular cues work even with one eye closed; most are also called pictorial cues because artists use them to suggest depth on a flat canvas. In linear perspective, parallel lines such as railway tracks seem to converge as they recede into the distance. In interposition (overlap), an object that blocks part of another is seen as nearer. Other monocular cues are relative size, texture gradient (texture looks finer with distance), aerial perspective (distant hills look hazy and bluish), light and shadow, height in the visual field and motion parallax (when we move, near objects seem to sweep past faster than far ones). Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different image, which the brain fuses into depth) and convergence (the eyes turn inward more for near objects). Example: Kabir covers one eye and looks down a long road lined with trees; the road narrows towards the horizon and nearer trees hide parts of farther ones, so he still sees depth. Option b is right because both cues work with a single eye and can be shown in a painting. Exam trap: options c and d are not separate categories but the two binocular cues themselves, placed to catch students who link every depth question to disparity and convergence.", "topic": "Monocular depth cues", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Vernier chronoscope is an instrument used to measure:", "options": {"a": "Perception", "b": "Sensation", "c": "Reaction time", "d": "Memory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the vernier chronoscope is an instrument used to measure reaction time. A chronoscope is any device for timing very short intervals, and in early psychology laboratories its main job was timing reaction time, the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the person's response. The best known was the Hipp chronoscope, built by the clockmaker Matthaus Hipp in the 1840s, a clockwork timer read in thousandths of a second that Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used in his Leipzig laboratory. Reaction time mattered because Franciscus Donders (1868) showed that subtracting a simple reaction time from a more complex one estimates how long a mental process takes, a method called mental chronometry. The vernier chronoscope, described by the American psychologist Edmund Sanford (1898), is a cheaper pendulum version. The stimulus releases one pendulum and the response releases a second, slightly faster one; counting the swings until the faster pendulum catches up gives the interval, read in the same way as the vernier scale on a calliper. Example: in a practical class, Meera presses a key as soon as a bell rings, and the chronoscope shows her simple reaction time, a fraction of a second. Option c is right because the chronoscope measures time, and reaction time is what psychology laboratories time with it. Exam trap: each other process has its own classic apparatus: the tachistoscope for brief exposures in perception and attention, the aesthesiometer for touch sensation and the memory drum for memory.", "topic": "Chronoscope and reaction time", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Most of the experimental work on Span of Attention was conducted by:", "options": {"a": "J.M. Cattell", "b": "Ebbinghaus", "c": "Wertheimer", "d": "Allport"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: most of the early experimental work on the span of attention was done by J. M. Cattell, that is, James McKeen Cattell. The span of attention (span of apprehension) is the number of separate items a person can grasp in a single brief glance, without counting. The question was first tried by William Stanley Jevons (1871), who threw handfuls of beans into a box and judged their number at a glance: he was never wrong with three or four. James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944), an American who took his doctorate with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig in 1886, studied the span systematically with the tachistoscope, a device that flashes a display for a tiny fraction of a second so that the eyes cannot move or count. He found that the span is small for unrelated letters but far larger when the letters form familiar words, and larger again when the words make a sentence, because meaningful material is grasped in bigger units. Later tachistoscope studies found that an adult grasps only about four to eight separate items in one glance, fewer for unrelated letters than for simple dots. Example: Arjun sees a card of random dots flashed for a tenth of a second and reports five correctly, but when the card shows a short familiar word he reads every letter. Option a is right because Cattell's tachistoscope experiments are the classic body of work on the span of attention. Exam trap: George Miller's (1956) 'seven, plus or minus two' is the span of short-term memory, a different process, and Hermann Ebbinghaus is a tempting pick because he too ran early experiments, but on memory.", "topic": "Span of attention", "fig": "James McKeen Cattell"}, {"q": "A researcher has obtained the data on the basis of gender and religion. This type of data is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Continuous series", "b": "Discrete series", "c": "Both (a) & (b)", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: data obtained on the basis of gender and religion form a discrete series. In statistics a series is a set of observations arranged for analysis. A discrete (discontinuous) series has separate, distinct values with nothing possible in between; such data are counted in whole numbers. A continuous series comes from a variable that can take any value within a range, including fractions, so it is measured rather than counted and is usually grouped into class intervals such as 150 to 155 cm. Gender and religion are categories: each person falls into exactly one class (male or female; Hindu, Muslim, Sikh, Christian), and the researcher can only count how many people fall into each. There is no value halfway between Sikh and Christian, and there cannot be 20.5 Hindus. Height, weight, age and reaction time, by contrast, are continuous. Example: Riya surveys 60 students and records 34 Hindus, 15 Muslims and 11 Sikhs; these frequencies are discrete counts, whereas the same students' heights in centimetres would form a continuous series. Option b is right because category data can only be counted in separate whole units, which is the mark of a discrete series. Exam trap: 'Both (a) and (b)' looks safe, but a variable with no in-between values at all cannot also be continuous. Key note: strictly, gender and religion are nominal (categorical) variables, which some texts call qualitative attributes rather than a numerical series; since nominal data are always discrete, discrete series is the best fit among the options, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Discrete and continuous series", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The mean and median of a given data is 7 and 12 respectively. What will be its mode?", "options": {"a": "19", "b": "5", "c": "9.5", "d": "22"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: when the mean is 7 and the median is 12, the mode is 22. For a moderately skewed distribution, Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) noted an empirical relation among the three averages: the median lies about one third of the way from the mean to the mode, which gives Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. Substituting the values: Mode = 3(12) minus 2(7) = 36 minus 14 = 22. The rule is called empirical because it was found by observing real distributions rather than derived as an exact law, so it holds only approximately and only for moderately skewed data. In a perfectly symmetrical (normal) distribution the mean, median and mode coincide. When they separate, their order shows the direction of skew: here the mean (7) is below the median (12), which is below the mode (22), so the long tail lies to the left and the distribution is negatively skewed. A few very low scores drag the mean down most, the median less and the mode not at all. Example: on an easy class test, most of Kabir's classmates score high, but a handful who barely attempted it score near zero, pulling the mean well below the median and mode. Option d is right because 3 times 12 minus 2 times 7 equals 22. Exam trap: the wrong options come from simpler operations on the same two numbers: 19 is mean plus median, 5 is median minus mean and 9.5 is their average, so a student who forgets the weights 3 and 2 lands on one of them.", "topic": "Empirical mode formula", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following graphical representation needs two extra intervals or classes?", "options": {"a": "Histogram", "b": "Frequency polygon", "c": "Frequency curve", "d": "Bar chart"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the frequency polygon needs two extra intervals or classes. A frequency distribution groups scores into class intervals (for example 20 to 24, 25 to 29) and records how many cases fall in each. To draw a frequency polygon, the midpoint of each interval is marked on the X axis, a point is plotted above it at the height of its frequency, and the points are joined by straight lines. If the line stopped at the first and last intervals, the figure would hang in the air above the baseline. So one extra interval with zero frequency is added below the lowest class and one above the highest, and the line is carried down to the baseline at their midpoints. This closes the figure and makes the area under the polygon equal to the area of the histogram for the same data, as Henry Garrett (1926) explains in Statistics in Psychology and Education. Polygons are especially useful for comparing two distributions on one graph. Example: Meera's test scores run from 20 to 49 in intervals of 5; she adds empty intervals of 15 to 19 and 50 to 54 so that her polygon starts and ends on the X axis. Option b is right because adding an empty class at each end is a defining step in drawing a frequency polygon. Exam trap: the histogram is drawn from the same table, but its bars stand on the exact class limits and need no extra classes. Key note: a smoothed frequency curve is often drawn from the closed polygon, so it can seem to use the extra intervals too, but the step of adding an empty interval at each end belongs to the frequency polygon itself, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Frequency polygon", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If the distribution is normal or near normal and the researcher is in need of further statistics from the measure of dispersion, then the most appropriate variability measure is:", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Average Deviation", "c": "Quartile Deviation", "d": "Standard Deviation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: when the distribution is normal or near normal and further statistics are needed, the most appropriate measure of variability is the standard deviation. Variability (dispersion) describes how spread out scores are around the average. The standard deviation (SD, sigma) is the square root of the mean of the squared deviations of scores from the mean: SD = square root of (sum of x squared divided by N), where x is each score minus the mean. Karl Pearson named it in lectures in 1893. Because it uses every score and squares the deviations instead of dropping their signs, it can be handled algebraically, and it is the most stable measure of spread from sample to sample. It is tied to the normal curve: about 68 per cent of cases fall within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. It is also the building block of further statistics: z scores and other standard scores, correlation, the t test, analysis of variance and reliability coefficients. Example: Arjun's and Riya's classes both average 60 marks, but Arjun's SD is 4 and Riya's is 15, so Riya's class is far more mixed; with the SD each mark can also be turned into a z score. Option d is right because the SD is the most reliable measure listed and the only one that feeds into higher statistics. Exam trap: quartile deviation is the right choice for the opposite case, skewed or open-ended data, where it partners the median; normal data go with the SD, skewed data with the quartile deviation.", "topic": "Standard deviation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The researcher has selected those participants who are interested to participate and will provide the richest information. He is using ______ sampling technique.", "options": {"a": "Random", "b": "Stratified", "c": "Purposive", "d": "Cluster"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the researcher is using purposive sampling, because he deliberately chose the people best placed to give rich information. Purposive (judgemental) sampling is a non-probability method: the researcher uses his own judgement about the aims of the study to hand-pick participants, instead of leaving selection to chance. Michael Quinn Patton (1990) described its logic as selecting 'information-rich cases', people from whom one can learn a great deal about the issues central to the research. It suits qualitative work, where depth matters more than generalisation, but the sample may not represent the population. In probability sampling, by contrast, every member of the population has a known chance of selection: simple random sampling gives everyone an equal chance; stratified random sampling divides the population into strata (such as gender or class) and draws randomly within each; cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups (schools, villages) and studies everyone in them. Example: Meera is studying how first-generation college students cope with English-medium classes. Instead of drawing names from the register, she approaches willing first-generation students who can describe their experience in detail. Option c is right because the choice was made deliberately, for the purpose of the study and the richness of information, not by chance. Exam trap: 'interested to participate' sounds like volunteer (self-selected) sampling, which is not offered; the deciding phrase is 'will provide the richest information', the hallmark of purposive sampling.", "topic": "Purposive sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The perfect negative correlation is represented by:", "options": {"a": "-100", "b": "+100", "c": "-1", "d": "+1"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a perfect negative correlation is written as -1 (strictly -1.00). A correlation coefficient describes the direction and strength of the linear relationship between two variables. Francis Galton (1888) described such 'co-relations', and Karl Pearson (1896) gave them an exact formula, the product-moment coefficient r. The coefficient always lies between -1.00 and +1.00. The sign shows the direction: a positive sign means both variables rise together; a negative sign means that as one rises, the other falls. The size shows the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and the closer the value is to 1 (either sign), the stronger the relationship. In a perfect correlation every point on the scatter plot falls exactly on a straight line, so one variable can be predicted from the other without error; when that line slopes downwards, r = -1. Example: Arjun starts the month with 600 rupees and spends exactly 20 rupees every day. The number of days passed and the money left correlate at exactly -1, because each extra day removes the same amount. Real psychological data almost never reach -1 or +1. Option c is right because -1 is the lower limit of the coefficient and marks a perfect inverse relationship. Exam trap: -100 and +100 look like percentages, but a correlation is not a percentage and can never exceed 1 in size. Also, a negative sign does not mean weak: r = -0.90 is a stronger relationship than r = +0.40.", "topic": "Range of correlation coefficient", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The critical values of \"t\" (in large sample) at 0.05 significance level are:", "options": {"a": "1.96 and 2.33", "b": "1.96 and 1.64", "c": "2.58 and 2.33", "d": "2.58 and 1.96"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in large samples the critical values of t at the 0.05 level are 1.96 (two-tailed) and 1.64 (one-tailed). The t distribution was published by William Sealy Gosset (1908), writing as 'Student' from the Guinness brewery in Dublin. It is bell-shaped like the normal curve but has heavier tails, so with few degrees of freedom (df) its critical values are larger: at df = 10 the two-tailed 0.05 value is 2.23. As df grow, the t distribution closes in on the normal curve, and with large samples (df above about 30, and certainly beyond 120) its critical values become the familiar z values. A critical value is the cut-off t must reach to reject the null hypothesis. At the 0.05 level, a two-tailed (non-directional) test splits the 5 per cent between both tails, 2.5 per cent each, giving 1.96; a one-tailed (directional) test puts the whole 5 per cent in one tail, giving 1.645, printed as 1.64 or 1.65. Example: Riya compares the reaction times of 200 athletes and 200 non-athletes. Her t of 2.10 passes 1.96, so the difference is significant at 0.05 two-tailed, but it falls short of 2.58, so it is not significant at 0.01. Option b is right because 1.96 and 1.64 are the two-tailed and one-tailed cut-offs at the 0.05 level. Exam trap: the four numbers are easy to swap. Learn them as pairs: 1.96 and 2.58 are two-tailed (0.05, 0.01); 1.64 and 2.33 are one-tailed. Key note: the paper prints 1.64, which is 1.645 rounded down; some texts round it to 1.65 instead, so b stands.", "topic": "Critical values of t", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Measurement is generally ______ whereas Evaluation is ______.", "options": {"a": "Quantitative, Qualitative", "b": "Qualitative, Quantitative", "c": "Subjective, Objective", "d": "True, False"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: measurement is generally quantitative, whereas evaluation is qualitative, because evaluation adds a value judgement to the numbers. Measurement is the assignment of numbers to objects or events according to rules, as Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) defined it. It answers the question 'how much?': a score of 42 out of 60, or a height of 120 cm. Measurement only describes. Evaluation is the wider process of judging worth or desirability. It asks 'how good?' or 'how far have the objectives been met?', and it uses test scores plus unmeasured information such as observation, interviews and anecdotal records. Norman E. Gronlund, whose Measurement and Evaluation in Teaching first appeared in 1965, summed this up in a well-known formula: evaluation equals quantitative description (measurement) and/or qualitative description (non-measurement), plus value judgements. So measurement is one narrower, more objective part of evaluation. Example: Riya scores 42 out of 60 in mathematics; that is measurement. Her teacher then writes, 'Riya has done well, has mastered fractions, needs more practice with word problems and is ready for the next unit'; that is evaluation. Option a is right because measurement yields numbers (quantitative) and evaluation interprets them with judgements of worth (qualitative). Exam trap: option b simply reverses the pair, and option c reverses it again in other words, since objectivity belongs mainly to measurement and subjective judgement to evaluation. Key note: evaluation also uses quantitative data, so it is not purely qualitative, but the standard contrast in Indian measurement texts is 'measurement is quantitative, evaluation is qualitative (judgemental)', so a stands.", "topic": "Measurement versus evaluation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first step in the construction of a psychological test is:", "options": {"a": "Writing items", "b": "Planning", "c": "Preliminary Administration", "d": "Developing Norms"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: planning is the first step in constructing a psychological test. The steps run in a fixed order. (1) Planning: the constructor fixes the purpose of the test, defines the trait or content to be measured, decides the target group, the type of items, the number of items, the time limit and the scoring method, and draws up a blueprint or table of specifications showing how many items will cover each area. (2) Item writing: far more items are written than will be kept. (3) Preliminary try-out: the draft is given to a sample like the target group. (4) Item analysis: each item's difficulty and discrimination are worked out, and weak items are revised or dropped. (5) Reliability is established. (6) Validity is established. (7) Norms are developed on a large, representative standardisation sample. (8) A manual is written describing administration, scoring and interpretation. Some Indian texts count seven steps, but all begin with planning. Example: Arjun wants to build an examination anxiety scale in Hindi for Class 10 students. Before writing a single statement, he must decide which aspects to cover (worry, bodily tension, avoidance), how many items each gets and the response format. Option b is right because every later step, from item writing to norms, follows from the decisions made at the planning stage. Exam trap: item writing feels like the obvious start because it is the first visible product, but it comes second; norms come near the end, after reliability and validity.", "topic": "Steps of test construction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The relationship between reliability and validity of a test is best represented by:", "options": {"a": "r12max = √(r11 × r12)", "b": "r12max = √(r11 × r22)", "c": "r11max = √r11 + √r12", "d": "r22max = √(r11 × r12)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the highest validity a test can reach is the square root of the product of the test's reliability and the criterion's reliability, r12max = √(r11 × r22). Here r12 is the validity coefficient (the correlation between the test, variable 1, and a criterion, variable 2), r11 is the reliability of the test and r22 the reliability of the criterion. Every obtained score is true score plus random error, and random error correlates with nothing, so only the true-score part of a test can correlate with anything outside it. The more error either measure carries, the lower the ceiling. If the criterion is perfectly reliable (r22 = 1), the formula shrinks to √r11, the index of reliability, which is the correlation between obtained and true scores. Run in reverse, the same reasoning gives the correction for attenuation of Charles Spearman (1904). Example: Kabir's aptitude test has a reliability of 0.81, and the supervisor ratings he uses as the criterion have a reliability of 0.64. The highest validity possible is √(0.81 × 0.64) = √0.5184 = 0.72; a reported validity of 0.85 would be impossible. Option b is right because it is the only formula that sets the validity ceiling from the two reliabilities. Exam trap: the rule runs one way only. A test can be reliable without being valid (a consistent measure of the wrong thing), but it cannot be valid without being reliable: reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity.", "topic": "Reliability limits validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The assessment of simultaneous relationship between learning disability test and school performance test is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Face validity", "b": "Predictive validity", "c": "Concurrent validity", "d": "Content validity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: correlating a learning disability test with school performance measured at the same time is an example of concurrent validity. In criterion-related validity, test scores are correlated with an outside measure of the same quality, called the criterion. Its two forms differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time, so the question is whether the test agrees with the person's present standing; it is often used to check a new test against an established measure or a current diagnosis. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later, so the question is whether the test forecasts future performance, as when an entrance test is correlated with first-year marks. The American Psychological Association's Technical Recommendations (1954) listed concurrent and predictive validity separately; later Standards grouped them together as criterion-related validity. Example: Meera, a school psychologist, gives her new learning disability screening test to 100 Class 4 children in the same week that their teachers record current marks in reading and arithmetic. If low test scores go with low current marks, the test shows concurrent validity. Option c is right because 'simultaneous' means the criterion is gathered at the same time as the test. Exam trap: predictive and concurrent validity use the same correlation, and the only difference is when the criterion is measured. Watch for 'simultaneous' or 'current' (concurrent) against 'later' or 'future' (predictive).", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When the internal consistency is the main source of error, then the most appropriate reliability method is:", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Split-half", "c": "Kappa statistics", "d": "Alternate forms/parallel forms"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: when inconsistency among the items is the main source of error, the split-half method is the most appropriate of these. Reliability is consistency of measurement, and each method of estimating it targets a different source of error. Error from time (mood or memory changing between occasions) calls for test-retest reliability, the same test given twice. Error from the particular set of items chosen calls for alternate (parallel) forms. Error from items within one test not measuring the same thing calls for internal consistency methods, which need one administration. In the split-half method the test is divided into two comparable halves, usually odd and even items, and the two half-scores are correlated. Because this gives the reliability of a test only half as long, the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) steps it up: full reliability = 2r / (1 + r). Other internal consistency methods are the Kuder-Richardson formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) and Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951). Example: Kabir gives his 40-item self-esteem scale once to 300 students, scores the odd and even items separately and finds that the halves correlate at 0.70; Spearman-Brown gives 2(0.70) / 1.70 = 0.82. Option b is right because split-half is the only internal consistency method among the options. Exam trap: kappa sounds technical, but Cohen's kappa measures agreement between raters, a different source of error (the scorer, not the items).", "topic": "Split-half internal consistency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Behaviouristic approach of personality mostly relies on:", "options": {"a": "Subjective techniques", "b": "Objective techniques", "c": "Projective techniques", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the behaviouristic approach to personality relies mostly on objective techniques. Behaviourists such as John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1953) treat personality as a set of learned responses shaped by the environment, not as hidden traits or unconscious drives, so they assess what a person actually does in observable, countable terms. Walter Mischel (1968) strengthened this view by showing that trait scores predict behaviour across situations only weakly. Behavioural assessment defines the target behaviour operationally ('hits a classmate'), then measures it directly: naturalistic or analogue observation, frequency and duration counts, behavioural checklists, self-monitoring diaries and ABC records of antecedent, behaviour and consequence. Subjective techniques (autobiography, introspective self-description, unstructured interviews) depend on the person's view of himself, and projective techniques (Rorschach, TAT) assume hidden motives show through responses to ambiguous material; humanistic and psychodynamic psychologists favour these. Example: to assess Arjun's classroom aggression, a behavioural psychologist does not show him inkblots. She watches him for a week, records each push or shout, notes what came just before (being teased) and just after (classmates laughing) and counts incidents per day. Option b is right because behaviourists measure observable behaviour objectively. Exam trap: projective tests carry the prestige of clinical psychology, but they belong to the psychodynamic approach, not to behaviourism. Key note: some texts call self-report inventories such as the MMPI and 16PF 'objective tests', although they are self-reports; the point of the item is that behaviourists rely on objective measurement of observable behaviour, so b stands.", "topic": "Behavioural assessment of personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term projective technique was coined by:", "options": {"a": "Francis Galton", "b": "Hermann Rorschach", "c": "Morgan and Murray", "d": "Lawrence Frank"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the term 'projective technique' (projective methods) was coined by Lawrence K. Frank (1939). In his paper 'Projective methods for the study of personality' in the Journal of Psychology, Frank grouped together tests in which a person meets an ambiguous, loosely structured stimulus and is free to respond in any way. Because the material has no fixed meaning, the person projects his own needs, conflicts, feelings and ways of seeing the world into the response. Frank likened these methods to an X-ray, which shows inner structure without opening the body. In psychoanalysis, projection was a defence: attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to someone else. The two best-known tests existed before Frank named the group: the inkblot test of Hermann Rorschach (1921), published in Psychodiagnostik, and the Thematic Apperception Test (1935) of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray. Example: shown a TAT picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced by his parents to practise. The theme of pressure comes from her, not from the picture, and that is what projective methods try to capture. Option d is right because Frank gave the whole family of tests its name. Exam trap: Hermann Rorschach created the most famous projective test, so he is the tempting answer, but creating a test is not coining the term; Rorschach himself presented his work as a diagnostic experiment in perception.", "topic": "Origin of projective techniques", "fig": "Lawrence K. Frank"}, {"q": "In the Rorschach Ink Blot Test (RIBT), Human (H), Animal (A) and Nature (N) are the types of:", "options": {"a": "Location", "b": "Determinant", "c": "Content", "d": "Form Quality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach Inkblot Test, Human (H), Animal (A) and Nature (N) are content categories. Hermann Rorschach (1921) devised ten symmetrical inkblots, five in black and grey and five with colour. The person says what each blot might be, then explains where and why in the inquiry. Each response is scored on several dimensions, worked out by Rorschach and refined by Samuel Beck (1944), Bruno Klopfer (1942) and John Exner, whose Comprehensive System appeared in 1974. Location asks where the percept was seen: W (whole blot), D (common detail), Dd (unusual detail), S (white space). Determinants ask which feature of the blot made it look that way: F (form), M (human movement), C (colour), shading and texture. Content asks what was seen: H (human), Hd (human detail), A (animal), Ad (animal detail), An (anatomy), N or Na (nature, such as landscapes), Sx (sex) and so on. Form quality asks how well the percept fits the shape of the blot (F+ good, F- poor). Example: on Card V Kabir says, 'The whole thing is a bat flying.' This is scored W for location, animal movement (FM) as the determinant, A for content, good form quality, and a popular response. Option c is right because H, A and N name what the response depicts, which is its content. Exam trap: location codes (W, D, Dd, S) and determinant codes (F, M, C) are also letters, so the abbreviations blur together; ask whether a code says where, why or what.", "topic": "Rorschach scoring categories", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Perceptual speed and accuracy is commonly emphasised in:", "options": {"a": "Scholastic Aptitude", "b": "Clerical Aptitude", "c": "Mechanical Aptitude", "d": "Vocational Aptitude"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: perceptual speed and accuracy is the ability most emphasised in clerical aptitude tests. An aptitude is a person's potential to succeed in a field with training; aptitude tests look forward, achievement tests look back. Clerical work, such as filing, checking accounts, copying records and data entry, needs a person to notice small differences quickly and without mistakes. So clerical tests usually present long lists of pairs of numbers or names and ask the examinee to mark, under a strict time limit, whether each pair is the same or different. The Minnesota Clerical Test, with number-checking and name-checking parts, is the classic example, and the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947) include a Clerical Speed and Accuracy subtest. These are speed tests: easy items, strict time. Example: Riya, applying for a bank clerk post, sees '4597312 4597312' and '68210945 68219045' and must mark 'same' or 'different' for hundreds of such pairs in a few minutes; only quick, careful checking catches the swapped digits in the second pair. Option b is right because clerical aptitude is defined by fast, accurate perception of detail. Exam trap: perceptual speed is also one of the primary mental abilities of Louis Thurstone (1938), so a general or scholastic answer can tempt, but scholastic aptitude tests stress verbal and numerical reasoning, not checking speed.", "topic": "Clerical aptitude tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which type of counselling emphasises more on intellectual aspects than emotional aspects?", "options": {"a": "Directive counselling", "b": "Non-directive counselling", "c": "Group counselling", "d": "Eclectic counselling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: directive counselling emphasises the intellectual aspects of a problem more than the emotional aspects. Directive (counsellor-centred) counselling was set out by Edmund G. Williamson (1939) in How to Counsel Students, the 'Minnesota point of view', building on the trait-factor tradition of Frank Parsons (1909). It assumes the client's difficulty comes mainly from a lack of information or poor reasoning, so the counsellor, as the expert, gathers data, reasons out the problem and advises. Its six steps are analysis (collecting data from tests, records and interviews), synthesis (organising the data into a picture of strengths and weaknesses), diagnosis (identifying the problem and its causes), prognosis (predicting the likely outcome), counselling (advising and helping the client adjust) and follow-up. Non-directive (client-centred) counselling, from Carl Rogers (1942) in Counseling and Psychotherapy, reverses the emphasis: feelings and emotional attitudes matter more than intellectual analysis, and the client leads while the counsellor offers empathy, acceptance and genuineness. Eclectic counselling, associated with Frederick Thorne (1950), mixes the two as each case requires. Example: Kabir cannot choose between science and commerce. A directive counsellor reviews his aptitude scores and marks, explains the prospects of each stream and recommends commerce, appealing to reason. Option a is right because directive counselling relies on rational analysis and expert advice. Exam trap: directive and non-directive are easily reversed; directive means intellect and a counsellor who leads, non-directive means feelings and a client who leads. Group counselling is a format (several clients at once), not a style defined by intellect or emotion, so it cannot be the answer.", "topic": "Directive counselling", "fig": "Edmund G. Williamson"}, {"q": "In the counselling situation the 'counsellee' is he:", "options": {"a": "Who undergoes counselling", "b": "Who undertakes counselling", "c": "Who conducts interview", "d": "Who takes care of client"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the counsellee is the person who undergoes counselling, the one who receives help. Counselling is a face-to-face helping relationship between two roles. The counsellor is the trained professional who conducts the sessions: builds rapport, listens, interviews, assesses and helps. The counsellee, usually called the client today, is the person who comes with a problem of living, such as a study difficulty, a career choice, a family conflict or worry, and works on it with the counsellor's help. The '-ee' ending marks the one who receives an action, as in employee or trainee, while '-or' marks the one who does it, as in employer or trainer. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, preferred the word 'client' to 'patient', because it suggests an active, responsible person who seeks help voluntarily rather than a sick person who is treated. The counsellee is usually a basically normal person with everyday problems of adjustment. Example: Meera, a Class 12 student anxious about her board examinations, meets the school counsellor every week. Meera is the counsellee; the school counsellor, who conducts the interviews and guides the sessions, is the counsellor. Option a is right because the counsellee is the one who undergoes, or receives, counselling. Exam trap: 'undertakes' and 'undergoes' look almost alike; to undertake counselling is to take on the job of giving it, which describes the counsellor.", "topic": "Counsellor and counsellee roles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In group counselling the role of counsellor is as:", "options": {"a": "Leader", "b": "Helper", "c": "Director", "d": "Follower"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in group counselling the counsellor acts as the group leader. Group counselling brings together a small group, usually about 6 to 10 members with similar concerns such as exam stress, homesickness or grief, to work on them with one or two trained counsellors. Members learn that others share their problems, get peer feedback and practise new behaviour safely. Irvin Yalom (1970) described these benefits as curative (now called therapeutic) factors, including universality, altruism, instillation of hope and interpersonal learning. Gerald Corey (1981), in Theory and Practice of Group Counseling, treats the counsellor in this setting as the group leader, who screens and prepares members, sets ground rules (such as confidentiality), facilitates interaction, links members' concerns, blocks harmful behaviour and models openness. A good group leader leads democratically: he guides the process but does not dictate what members say or decide. Example: Arjun, a college counsellor, runs an eight-member group for first-year hostel students who feel homesick. He sets the rules, invites quiet members to speak, links Riya's loneliness to similar feelings voiced by two others and sums up at the end. As leader he keeps the group safe and moving, while the members help each other. Option a is right because 'leader' is the standard name for the counsellor's role in a group. Exam trap: 'director' sounds close, but it implies authoritarian control, which group counselling avoids. Key note: some guidance texts describe the group counsellor as a helper or facilitator; a facilitating leader is still a leader, and leader is the standard role name in group counselling, so a stands.", "topic": "Counsellor's role in groups", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first step in the counselling process is:", "options": {"a": "Administering tools", "b": "Discussing problem", "c": "Building rapport", "d": "Solving client's problem"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first step in the counselling process is building rapport, a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor builds it by greeting the client warmly, giving full attention (eye contact, an open posture, nodding), listening without judging, reflecting feelings, explaining what counselling involves and promising confidentiality within its limits. Carl Rogers (1957) listed six necessary and sufficient conditions for change, starting with psychological contact between counsellor and client; the counsellor's part is empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness. Gerard Egan (1975), in The Skilled Helper, likewise opens the helping process with attending to the client and helping him explore his concerns. Indian guidance texts give the process as building rapport, exploring the problem, assessment (including tests where needed), setting goals, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without trust, clients hide or soften the real problem, so later steps rest on half the facts. Example: Farah, a Class 9 student, is sent to the school counsellor for missing classes. Asked at once 'Why are you bunking?', Farah says nothing. If the counsellor first chats about Farah's favourite subject, listens patiently and promises confidentiality, Farah soon admits that she is being teased on the school bus. Option c is right because exploring, testing and problem solving all depend on the trust built at the start. Exam trap: discussing the problem feels like the obvious opening, since the client came with a problem, but real discussion begins only once rapport exists; and solving the problem is not a step the counsellor performs for the client.", "topic": "First step of counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Counselling services are required:", "options": {"a": "At elementary level", "b": "At secondary level", "c": "At middle level", "d": "Throughout the life"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: counselling services are needed throughout life, not at one school level only. Guidance and counselling are described as a continuous, lifelong process, because every stage of life brings its own choices and problems of adjustment. At the elementary level children need help settling into school, building study habits and coping with learning difficulties, fears or trouble at home. At the middle level, early adolescence brings peer pressure and questions of identity. At the secondary level students choose subjects and streams, prepare for board examinations and begin to think about careers. Adults face jobs, marriage, illness, loss and retirement. Developmental psychology supports this view: Erik Erikson (1950) described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age, each with its own crisis, and Donald Super (1957) treated career development as a life-span process running from growth and exploration through establishment and maintenance to decline (later called disengagement). Example: Kabir sees a school counsellor in Class 3 for reading problems, a career counsellor in Class 11 to choose between science and commerce, a workplace counsellor at 35 for job stress and a counsellor again at 62 when he struggles to adjust to retirement. Option d is right because the need for counselling arises at every stage, from childhood to old age. Exam trap: because Indian texts discuss guidance mainly within schools, the secondary level, where subject and career choices are made, looks like the answer; but a basic principle of guidance is continuity, so it is needed throughout the life span.", "topic": "Lifelong need for counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Guidance is:", "options": {"a": "Improving the quality of relationship with each other", "b": "Promotion of the utilisation of one's own potential", "c": "Helping the individual to change one's personality", "d": "All the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: guidance is the promotion of the use of one's own potential, helping a person discover and develop his abilities and make his own choices. Guidance is a process of helping individuals understand themselves (their abilities, interests and limits) and their opportunities, so that they can make wise educational, vocational and personal decisions and direct their own lives. Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (1951), in An Introduction to Guidance, stress that guidance is not giving directions or imposing one's views: it helps a person of any age manage his own life, develop his own points of view, make his own decisions and carry his own burdens. Other classic definitions agree: guidance helps each person, through his own efforts, to discover and develop his potentialities. So guidance is for everyone, is continuous, respects the right to choose and aims at self-direction. Example: Meera scores high on a mechanical aptitude test and enjoys repairing gadgets, but has never thought of engineering. Her guidance teacher shows her the results, discusses courses and careers with her and leaves the decision to her. Meera can now use a potential she had not recognised. Option b is right because guidance aims at the fullest use of a person's own abilities. Exam trap: 'All the above' tempts because guidance often does improve relationships, but guidance does not try to change anyone's personality; it works with the person as he is. Key note: guidance can improve relationships too, but 'all the above' fails because guidance does not set out to change personality, so b stands.", "topic": "Meaning of guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who is known as father of guidance movement in American education?", "options": {"a": "E.G. Williamson", "b": "Frank Parsons", "c": "J.B. Davis", "d": "John Brewer"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908) is known as the father of the guidance movement in American education. Parsons, a Boston engineer, lawyer and social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people, many of them immigrants, choose work wisely instead of drifting into whatever jobs they found. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, a year after his death. In it he set out three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, interests, limitations); knowledge of the requirements, advantages and prospects of different kinds of work; and 'true reasoning' on the relations between these two groups of facts. This matching of person and job became the trait-factor approach, later systematised by Edmund G. Williamson (1939) as directive counselling. Example: following Parsons, a counsellor helping Arjun would first assess his abilities and interests, then gather facts about careers such as nursing, accountancy and electrical work, and finally reason out with him which job fits him best. Option b is right because Parsons founded organised vocational guidance and is the figure standard texts call the father of the guidance movement. Exam trap: Jesse B. Davis (Vocational and Moral Guidance, 1914) brought guidance lessons into American classrooms, so he is a tempting 'education' answer, and Edmund G. Williamson is famous for directive counselling, a later development. Key note: Jesse B. Davis built guidance into the school curriculum first, and some American texts honour him as the first school counsellor, but Frank Parsons is the figure standard texts call the father of the guidance movement, so b stands.", "topic": "Father of guidance movement", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not the ethical principle of counselling?", "options": {"a": "Respect for autonomy", "b": "Beneficence", "c": "Nonmaleficence", "d": "Obligation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: obligation is not one of the ethical principles of counselling. Karen Kitchener (1984), writing in The Counseling Psychologist, proposed five moral principles as the foundation for ethical decisions in counselling. Respect for autonomy means honouring the client's right to make his own choices, as long as they do not harm others; it underlies informed consent and confidentiality. Beneficence means doing good: acting for the client's welfare and offering competent help. Nonmaleficence means doing no harm, including avoiding harmful techniques and exploitative dual relationships. Justice means fairness: treating clients equally and giving fair access to services. Fidelity means keeping promises and being trustworthy. Kitchener added fidelity to the four biomedical principles of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979); the American Counseling Association's code later added veracity (truthfulness). Example: Riya, a counsellor, learns that her client Arjun wants to leave engineering for music. Respecting his autonomy, she helps him weigh the choice but does not decide for him; she keeps his disclosures private as promised (fidelity), refers him on if his low mood worsens beyond her competence (beneficence and nonmaleficence), and gives him the same time and care as her other clients (justice). Option d is right because 'obligation' names none of the principles in these lists. Exam trap: counsellors certainly have obligations, and duties run through every code, so 'obligation' sounds ethical; but it is a general word for duty, not a named principle.", "topic": "Ethical principles of counselling", "fig": "Karen Kitchener"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2011": [{"q": "Individual's potential for growth and the role of unique perception in guiding behaviour and mental processes is emphasised in:", "concept": "The answer is d: the humanistic approach emphasises a person's potential for growth and his or her unique, subjective perception of the world as the guides of behaviour and mental processes. Humanistic psychology grew in the 1950s and 1960s as the 'third force', a reaction against psychoanalysis, which stressed unconscious conflict, and behaviourism, which stressed conditioning. Its two leading founders were Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1951). Maslow argued that once lower needs are met, people strive towards self-actualisation, becoming everything they are capable of being. Rogers argued that each person lives in a private phenomenal field, the world as he or she experiences it, and that behaviour follows this perceived world rather than the objective one. This focus on conscious personal experience is called phenomenology. Example: Meera and Arjun get the same marks in an exam. Meera sees the result as a sign that she can grow, so she joins a new course; Arjun sees it as failure and withdraws. A humanistic psychologist explains their different behaviour through their different perceptions and their drive towards growth. Option d is right because 'potential for growth' is self-actualisation and 'unique perception' is the phenomenal field, the two pillars of the humanistic approach. Exam trap: the psychodynamic approach also deals with inner mental life, so it tempts, but it stresses unconscious conflicts and early childhood, not conscious perception and growth; the behaviouristic approach is the one most opposed to the stem, because it leaves inner experience out altogether.", "traps": {"a": "The psychodynamic approach of Sigmund Freud explains behaviour by unconscious conflicts among id, ego and superego rooted in early childhood; it is pessimistic about human nature and does not centre on growth or conscious, unique perception.", "b": "The biological approach explains behaviour through the brain, nervous system, hormones and genes; it treats perception as a product of physiology, not as a personal viewpoint that steers growth.", "c": "The behaviouristic approach of John B. Watson and B. F. Skinner studies only observable behaviour shaped by conditioning and reinforcement; it deliberately ignores inner perception and self-directed growth."}}, {"q": "When a researcher is using questionnaires to poll large groups of people, he is adopting:", "concept": "The answer is d: a researcher who uses questionnaires to poll large groups of people is using the survey method. A survey collects self-reports, through written questionnaires or structured interviews, from a large sample in order to describe the attitudes, opinions, beliefs or behaviour of a population. Fred N. Kerlinger (1910 to 1991) defined survey research as studying populations by selecting samples, to discover the incidence, distribution and interrelations of psychological and sociological variables. Its weaknesses are that people may give careless or socially desirable answers, the wording of questions can bias replies, and a sample that does not represent the population gives misleading results. Famous examples are the opinion polls of George Gallup (1901 to 1984), who correctly forecast the 1936 United States presidential election with a far smaller but better chosen sample than the Literary Digest's huge mail poll, and the Kinsey reports on sexual behaviour by Alfred Kinsey (1948, 1953). Example: Kavya wants to know how Indian college students feel about online classes, so she emails a 20-item questionnaire to 2,000 students across ten universities. Option d is right because questionnaires given to large groups are the defining tool of the survey method. Exam trap: survey data are often used to calculate correlations, so students pick 'correlational method'; but the stem describes how the data are collected, by asking many people, which is the survey, not the analysis of how two variables relate.", "traps": {"a": "The correlational method measures two or more variables as they naturally occur and calculates how strongly they go together (for example, hours of study and marks); it is about analysing a relationship, not the act of polling large groups.", "b": "The observational method records behaviour by watching it, in natural settings or the laboratory, without asking people questions; questionnaires are self-report, not observation.", "c": "The clinical method is an intensive study of one individual through interviews, tests and case history (Sigmund Freud's cases; Jean Piaget's clinical interview with children); it studies one person in depth, not large groups."}}, {"q": "Curt Stern's \"Rubber Band\" analogy is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: Curt Stern's rubber band analogy shows that heredity and environment act together, so it is associated with both. Curt Stern (1902 to 1981) was a German-born American geneticist. He compared a person's genetic endowment for a trait such as height or intelligence to a rubber band. Heredity decides what kind of band you have: its unstretched length and how far it could possibly stretch. Environment decides how much it is actually stretched. A short band pulled hard by a rich environment can end up longer than a long band left slack in a poor one. So the visible trait (the phenotype) is never the product of genes alone or of environment alone, but of their interaction. The idea is close to the reaction range of Irving Gottesman (1963): genes set upper and lower limits, and the environment decides where within those limits a person lands. Example: Arjun and Kabir are born with similar genetic potential. Arjun gets good nutrition, books and coaching, while Kabir grows up malnourished with little schooling, so Arjun's measured intelligence is higher even though their 'bands' were alike. Option c is right because the whole purpose of the analogy is that genes fix the potential and the environment determines how much of it is realised. Exam trap: the band itself stands for the genes, so examiners hope you will answer 'heredity' alone. Key note: some guides key 'Heredity' because the band represents the genotype, but the analogy exists to show that heredity and environment jointly set the outcome, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Heredity alone is only half the analogy: the rubber band stands for the genetic potential, but the point of the analogy is that the environment decides how far that band is stretched.", "b": "Environment alone is the other half: the stretching force is environmental, but it can only work within the limits that the inherited band allows.", "d": "Behaviour is an outcome that the two forces shape, not what the analogy is about; the analogy explains where traits come from, the interaction of heredity and environment."}}, {"q": "Which form of child discipline tends to make children insecure, anxious and hungry for approval?", "concept": "The answer is c: withdrawal of love is the form of discipline that tends to make children insecure, anxious and hungry for approval. Martin Hoffman, in work with Herbert Saltzstein (1967), sorted parental discipline into three techniques. Power assertion uses force, threats, physical punishment or removal of privileges. Love withdrawal shows anger or disappointment by withholding affection: ignoring the child, refusing to speak, turning away or threatening to leave. Induction explains how the child's act affects other people, and it best builds empathy, guilt and an inner conscience. Under love withdrawal the child often obeys, but because affection now seems conditional, he or she becomes anxious, insecure and eager to win approval back, instead of learning why the act was wrong. Power assertion, by contrast, is linked with hostility, aggression and weak moral internalisation. Example: when six-year-old Riya spills juice, her mother stops talking to her for the whole evening. Riya follows her mother around, apologising and asking 'Are you still angry?', the anxious, approval-hungry pattern of love withdrawal. Option c is right because the stem's three outcomes, insecurity, anxiety and craving for approval, are the textbook effects of withholding affection. Exam trap: 'authoritarian' and 'authoritative' look like discipline answers, but they are Diana Baumrind's (1966) parenting styles, broad climates of warmth and control, not specific discipline techniques. Key note: Baumrind's authoritarian children are also described as anxious and withdrawn, but 'hungry for approval' is the classic marker of love withdrawal and the stem asks for a form of discipline, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Authoritarian is one of Diana Baumrind's parenting styles (high control, low warmth, 'because I said so'); it is linked with withdrawn, less confident children, but it is an overall style, not the discipline technique that breeds hunger for approval.", "b": "Power assertion is Martin Hoffman's term for discipline by force, threats and punishment; it is linked with hostility, aggression and obedience only when watched, not with an approval-seeking child.", "d": "Authoritative is Baumrind's warm but firm style that explains its rules; it produces the most self-reliant, socially competent and secure children, the opposite of the stem."}}, {"q": "Learning that is not demonstrated at the time it occurs is:", "concept": "The answer is d: learning that is not demonstrated at the time it occurs is latent learning. Latent means hidden. In latent learning a person or animal learns something without any reward, but the learning stays out of sight until there is a reason to use it. The classic evidence comes from Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930). Three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze every day. One group always found food in the goal box, one never did, and the third found no food for the first ten days and then found food from day 11. Once food appeared, the third group's errors dropped almost at once to the level of the group rewarded all along, and soon even below it. These rats must have learned the maze during the unrewarded days, forming what Tolman called a cognitive map, and simply had no reason to show it. The study challenged the strict behaviourist view that reinforcement is needed for learning: reinforcement affects performance, not learning itself. Example: Kabir rides as a passenger to college every day and never drives. One day his friend falls ill, and Kabir drives the route without a single wrong turn; he had learned it all along. Option d is right because the defining feature of latent learning is the gap between learning and its display. Exam trap: observational learning is also often shown later, which tempts students, but the term for learning that stays hidden until an incentive appears is latent learning.", "traps": {"a": "Insight learning is Wolfgang Kohler's term for a sudden solution after the problem is mentally restructured, as when his chimpanzee Sultan joined two sticks to reach a banana; it is shown the moment it happens, not kept hidden.", "b": "Social learning is Albert Bandura's theory that people learn by watching others and the consequences others receive; it names the source of learning (other people), not the fact that learning stays hidden.", "c": "Observational learning is learning by watching a model, shown in Bandura's Bobo doll study (1961); it can be displayed later, but the term names learning by watching, while the hidden-until-needed quality is latent learning (Bandura called the gap the learning-performance distinction)."}}, {"q": "When a child learns to fear the doctor's clinic by associating it with the reflexive emotional reaction to a painful injection, the process is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: when a child learns to fear the clinic because it has been paired with a painful injection, the process is acquisition, the first stage of classical conditioning. Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed that when a neutral stimulus is paired repeatedly with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) that naturally triggers a reflex, the neutral stimulus alone comes to trigger a similar response. Here the injection is the UCS, the reflexive pain and fear is the unconditioned response (UCR), the clinic becomes the conditioned stimulus (CS), and fear of the clinic is the conditioned response (CR). Acquisition is the period in which this CS and UCS link is formed and the CR grows stronger with each pairing, fastest when the CS comes just before the UCS. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) used the same process to condition fear in the infant Little Albert, pairing a white rat with a loud noise. Example: after two painful vaccinations, three-year-old Ishaan starts crying as soon as the car turns into the clinic's car park, before any needle is in sight. Option c is right because the stem describes the fear being learned for the first time through pairing. Exam trap: generalisation, discrimination and extinction all act on a CR that already exists; the stem says nothing about similar stimuli, about telling stimuli apart or about the fear fading.", "traps": {"a": "Stimulus generalisation is the spread of an existing CR to stimuli similar to the CS, as when Little Albert's fear spread to a rabbit and a fur coat; it would fit if the child came to fear every hospital, not the first learning of the fear.", "b": "Stimulus discrimination is learning to respond to the CS but not to similar stimuli, for example fearing the clinic but not the school nurse's room; it refines a CR after acquisition.", "d": "Extinction is the gradual weakening of a CR when the CS is presented again and again without the UCS, for example clinic visits with no injections; the stem describes fear being learned, not lost."}}, {"q": "Chunking technique is used to enhance the capacity of:", "concept": "The answer is b: chunking is used to increase the capacity of short-term memory. Short-term memory (STM) holds a small amount of information for about 15 to 30 seconds unless it is rehearsed. George Miller (1956), in his paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', showed that its span is about 7 plus or minus 2 units, whether the units are digits, letters or words. The crucial point is that the limit is set in units, not in raw pieces of information. Chunking means grouping separate items into larger meaningful units, so that each chunk fills only one slot. The more a person knows, the bigger the chunks can be: a chess master sees a whole board as a few familiar patterns. Example: Riya must remember the digits 9 8 4 5 6 3 2 1 0 7. Ten separate digits exceed most people's span, but grouped like a mobile number, 98456 32107, they become two chunks. Likewise the letters I N D I A I I T I S R O become three chunks: INDIA, IIT, ISRO. Option b is right because chunking works around the small number of slots, the defining bottleneck of short-term memory. Exam trap: chunking also helps later storage, so 'long-term memory' tempts, but long-term memory has no known capacity limit to enlarge; the capacity problem chunking solves belongs to short-term memory.", "traps": {"a": "Sensory memory briefly holds a large, raw copy of input (iconic memory lasts well under a second, as George Sperling showed in 1960); its capacity is already large, and chunking needs meaning that is applied only once information reaches short-term memory.", "c": "Long-term memory is the relatively permanent store with no known capacity limit, so there is no capacity to enlarge; chunking helps material enter it but does not expand it.", "d": "Episodic memory, Endel Tulving's (1972) term for memory of personal events in time and place, is a part of long-term memory, so it shares long-term memory's unlimited capacity."}}, {"q": "Achievement and recognition are ______ needs whereas affiliation and acceptance are ______ needs:", "concept": "The answer is d: achievement and recognition are esteem needs, whereas affiliation and acceptance are belongingness needs. Abraham Maslow (1943) arranged human needs in a hierarchy of five levels. From the bottom they are: physiological needs (food, water, sleep, warmth); safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear); belongingness and love needs (affiliation, friendship, acceptance, being part of a group); esteem needs (achievement, competence, mastery, recognition, status, respect from others and self-respect); and self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming. Lower needs are more pressing, or prepotent: a starving person thinks of food, not fame. The first four levels are deficiency needs, felt when something is missing, and self-actualisation is a growth need. Example: Arjun has just moved to Pune for a job. First he wants to make friends and be accepted by his colleagues (belongingness). Once he feels part of the team, he works for a promotion and the 'employee of the month' award (esteem). Option d is right because achievement and recognition concern competence and respect, the esteem level, while affiliation and acceptance concern being loved and included, the belongingness level just below it. Exam trap: option c shifts each label one level too high, putting self-actualisation where esteem belongs and esteem where belongingness belongs.", "traps": {"a": "Physiological needs are bodily needs such as hunger, thirst and sleep, and safety needs are security and freedom from danger; neither covers achievement or social acceptance.", "b": "Safety needs concern security and stability, not achievement; belongingness is right for the second blank, but the first blank should be esteem.", "c": "Self-actualisation is realising one's full potential, not winning recognition, and esteem is the level of achievement and recognition, not of affiliation; each label is one level too high."}}, {"q": "\"I am afraid because I am aroused\" explains:", "concept": "The answer is a: 'I am afraid because I am aroused' expresses the James-Lange theory of emotion. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885), working independently, reversed the common sense order of emotion. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. James argued that we see the bear, tremble and run, and the feeling of fear is our awareness of those bodily changes: we are afraid because we tremble. So the stimulus produces bodily arousal first, and the felt emotion is the perception of that arousal. This implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily change. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory, arguing that the internal organs react too slowly and too similarly across emotions to produce distinct feelings. Example: Meera is walking home at night and hears footsteps behind her. Her heart pounds and her legs shake; on the James-Lange view, sensing the pounding and shaking is what makes her feel afraid. Option a is right because the stem puts arousal first and treats fear as the result of that arousal. Exam trap: the two-factor theory also contains arousal, so 'cognitive arousal theory' tempts; but there arousal alone is not enough, and fear needs a cognitive label for the arousal, whereas the stem has no label, only 'because I am aroused'.", "traps": {"b": "The Cannon-Bard theory of Walter Cannon (1927) and Philip Bard holds that the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same moment, so feeling and arousal occur together and independently; arousal does not cause the fear.", "c": "Cognitive arousal theory (the two-factor theory) of Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) says emotion is arousal plus a cognitive label taken from the situation ('I am aroused and the dark street explains why, so this is fear'); arousal alone does not decide the emotion.", "d": "Common sense theory says the emotion comes first and then causes the bodily reaction ('I am afraid, so I tremble'), the reverse of the stem."}}, {"q": "Both mental age and chronological age of Aisha is 12. What will be her IQ?", "concept": "The answer is c: when Aisha's mental age and chronological age are both 12, her IQ is 100. The ratio IQ compares mental age (MA), the age level of the test items a child can pass, with chronological age (CA), her actual age. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing MA by CA, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the result by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to remove decimals: IQ = (MA / CA) x 100. For Aisha: (12 / 12) x 100 = 1 x 100 = 100. An IQ of 100 means the child performs exactly at the level typical of her age, neither ahead nor behind. If MA is above CA, the IQ is above 100 (MA 15 at CA 12 gives 125); if MA is below CA, it is below 100 (MA 9 at CA 12 gives 75). The ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops rising in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, which has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. On that scale too, average performance for one's age is 100. Option c is right because equal mental and chronological ages give a ratio of 1, and 1 x 100 is 100. Exam trap: '12' tempts because both ages are 12, but 12 is the mental age or the chronological age, not the quotient; the IQ is the ratio multiplied by 100.", "traps": {"a": "120 would need a mental age above the chronological age, for example a mental age of 14.4 at age 12 (14.4 / 12 x 100 = 120); with equal ages the IQ cannot exceed 100.", "b": "12 is simply Aisha's mental age (or her chronological age); the formula divides one by the other and multiplies by 100, so 12 is not her IQ.", "d": "'Between 100 and 120' would mean she is somewhat ahead of her age group; equal ages give exactly 100, not a range."}}, {"q": "Thinking that produces many ideas or alternatives is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: thinking that produces many ideas or alternatives is divergent thinking. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, argued that intelligence tests ignored creativity because they rewarded only one kind of thinking. He separated convergent thinking, which narrows information down to the single correct answer, from divergent thinking, which branches out from one starting point to many possible answers; in his Structure of Intellect model he called it divergent production. Divergent thinking is scored on fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (number of different categories), originality (how unusual the ideas are) and elaboration (how much detail is added). The classic task is Alternate Uses: 'list all the uses of a brick'. Ellis Paul Torrance later built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) on the same idea. Example: asked for uses of a newspaper, Kavya lists reading, wrapping, a fan, a paper boat, a floor mat and a mask for a school play; her long, varied list shows divergent thinking. Option a is right because 'producing many ideas or alternatives' is the exact definition of divergent thinking. Exam trap: 'creative thinking' sounds right, but creativity is the broader outcome and also needs ideas to be judged and refined. Key note: divergent thinking is the core of creativity, so 'creative thinking' is close, but the specific term for producing many ideas or alternatives is divergent thinking, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Convergent thinking, Guilford's opposite term, narrows information to the one correct or best answer, as on IQ and achievement tests; it produces a single answer, not many.", "c": "Critical thinking is evaluating claims, arguments and evidence to decide which are sound; it judges ideas rather than generating many of them.", "d": "Creative thinking is the broad process of producing ideas that are both new and useful, which also needs convergent judgement; divergent thinking is the specific part that produces many alternatives."}}, {"q": "Guilford's SOI model contains:", "concept": "The answer is a: Guilford's Structure of Intellect (SOI) model contains 4 contents x 6 products x 5 operations, giving 120 abilities. Joy Paul Guilford (1967), in The Nature of Human Intelligence, rejected a single general intelligence and pictured intellect as a cube with three faces. Each ability is one cell of the cube, defined by one choice from each face. Operations are what the mind does: cognition, memory, divergent production, convergent production and evaluation (5). Contents are what it works on: figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural (4). Products are the form the result takes: units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications (6). So 4 x 6 x 5 = 120 separate abilities. A memory aid is COP, 4 5 6: Contents 4, Operations 5, Products 6. Example: when Rohan spots the odd word out in 'mango, apple, potato, banana', he is using cognition (operation) of semantic (content) classes (product), one cell of the cube. Option a is right because it is the only option that gives the correct number for every face of the 1967 model. Exam trap: option b keeps the total of 120 but swaps two numbers, giving 5 products and 6 operations, so check each face, not just the product. Key note: Guilford later split figural content into visual and auditory (150 abilities) and memory into memory recording and memory retention (180 abilities); none of the options matches those later versions, so the classic 1967 model and the answer used here stand.", "traps": {"b": "4 contents x 5 products x 6 operations swaps two numbers: Guilford had 6 products (units to implications) and 5 operations (cognition to evaluation), not the other way round.", "c": "5 contents x 4 products x 6 operations is wrong on every face; five contents belongs only to a later revision (figural split into visual and auditory), and Guilford never had just four products.", "d": "6 contents x 5 products x 4 operations inverts the model; there are 4 contents, 6 products and 5 operations."}}, {"q": "A social learning process whereby individuals come to internalize or accept their own culture or subculture patterns is termed as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the social learning process by which individuals come to internalise and accept the patterns of their own culture or subculture is socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong process through which a person learns the norms, values, beliefs, roles, language and customs of his or her society, and comes to hold them as his or her own rather than merely obeying them. It turns a newborn into a functioning member of a group. The family is the chief agent in early childhood (primary socialisation); school, peers, religion, the workplace and the mass media follow (secondary socialisation). It works through instruction, reward and punishment, and imitation of models. Adopting a group's norms before joining it is anticipatory socialisation, a term of Robert Merton (1910 to 2003); unlearning old norms and learning new ones after a major change, such as joining the army, is resocialisation. Example: Ananya grows up in a Tamil family in Chennai. Without formal lessons she learns to touch elders' feet, to eat with her right hand and to celebrate Pongal, and as an adult she follows these customs because they feel right to her, not because anyone tells her to. Option c is right because the stem's key words, learning and internalising one's culture's patterns, define socialisation. Exam trap: 'social facilitation' also begins with 'social' and sounds like a learning process, but it concerns performance in front of others, not absorbing a culture.", "traps": {"a": "A group is two or more people who interact, share goals and influence one another; it is one setting in which socialisation happens, not the process of learning a culture.", "b": "Society is the large, organised community of people who share a territory and a culture; it is what the individual is socialised into, not the learning process itself.", "d": "Social facilitation, first studied by Norman Triplett (1898) and explained by Robert Zajonc (1965), is better performance on easy or well-learned tasks when others are present; it concerns performance, not internalising culture."}}, {"q": "The most suitable method for understanding the social taboos is:", "concept": "The answer is c: observation is the most suitable method for understanding social taboos. A taboo is a strong, usually unwritten prohibition backed by a sense of the sacred, disgust or shame: rules about what may not be eaten, touched, said or done, and with whom. Taboos are hard to study by asking. People are reluctant to talk about forbidden subjects, often cannot put unspoken rules into words, and tend to give polite or socially desirable answers. What taboos do leave is a trace in behaviour: what people avoid, how they react when a rule is broken, which places and objects are kept apart. So researchers watch real behaviour in natural settings, usually through participant observation, in which the observer lives with the group. This is the classic method of anthropology, made famous by the fieldwork of Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) in the Trobriand Islands. Example: Sneha wants to understand menstrual taboos in a village. Women give short, guarded answers in interviews, but by living there for months she sees who enters the kitchen and temple, who sleeps apart and how others respond. Option c is right because observation records what people actually do and avoid, without relying on what they are willing to say. Exam trap: the interview looks like the natural way to learn about beliefs, but taboo subjects are exactly the ones people avoid discussing. Key note: interviews can add meaning once trust exists, but observation of real behaviour is the method texts single out for taboos, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "A case study is an in-depth study of one person, family or unit over time; it is too narrow to reveal a norm shared by a whole community, and it still depends heavily on what the case reports.", "b": "An interview gathers information by asking questions face to face; it is weakest exactly where taboos lie, because people avoid or soften talk about forbidden subjects and give socially desirable replies.", "d": "The experimental method manipulates an independent variable under controlled conditions; a taboo cannot be created or manipulated, and breaking one on purpose would be unethical."}}, {"q": "Charismatic leaders are also known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: charismatic leaders are also known as transformational leaders. The sociologist Max Weber (1922) described charismatic authority, power that rests on followers' devotion to a leader seen as exceptional, unlike traditional authority (a king) or legal-rational authority (an official). Robert House (1977) turned this into a theory of charismatic leadership: such leaders voice a vision, show strong confidence and conviction, model the behaviour they want and set high expectations, and followers trust and identify with them. James MacGregor Burns (1978) contrasted transforming leaders, who raise followers' motives and values, with transactional leaders, who exchange rewards for effort. Bernard Bass (1985) developed transformational leadership further, and it is now usually described by four components: idealised influence (charisma), inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation and individualised consideration. Because charisma is its central component, textbooks treat the two labels as near synonyms. Example: at a struggling Bengaluru start-up, the founder Nikhil shares a vivid vision of affordable health care for villages; his team works late not for bonuses but because they believe in him and the mission. Option c is right because charisma is the heart of transformational leadership: both describe leaders who inspire followers to go beyond self-interest. Exam trap: 'autocratic' and 'democratic' are familiar leadership labels too, but they come from a different scheme, styles of making decisions, and say nothing about inspiration or vision.", "traps": {"a": "Autocratic leaders, one of the three styles studied by Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939), make every decision alone and give orders; this describes how decisions are made, not the inspiring vision of a charismatic leader.", "b": "Democratic leaders, from the same Lewin, Lippitt and White (1939) study, involve the group in decisions; the style concerns participation, and a democratic leader need not be charismatic.", "d": "'Unique leaders' is not a recognised category in any leadership theory; it is a filler option that plays on the idea that charismatic people seem special."}}, {"q": "In communication process radio, T.V., mobile phones are all examples of:", "concept": "The answer is d: radio, television and mobile phones are all channels in the communication process. Communication is the transfer of a message from one person to another, and the classic models break it into parts. Claude Shannon and Warren Weaver (1949) described a source, a transmitter, a channel, noise, a receiver and a destination. David Berlo (1960) simplified this into the SMCR model: Source, Message, Channel, Receiver. The source, or sender, has an idea and encodes it into a message of words, pictures or signals. The channel is the medium or pathway through which the message travels: face to face speech, a letter, a telephone line, radio, television, the internet. The receiver decodes the message, and feedback is the receiver's response sent back to the source, which turns a one-way process into a two-way one. Example: Pooja's college announces exam dates on the notice board, in a WhatsApp group and on local radio. The exam office is the sender, the dates are the message, Pooja and her classmates are the receivers, and the notice board, WhatsApp and radio are the channels. Option d is right because radio, TV and phones are not people who send or receive, but media that carry the message between them. Exam trap: because a phone or TV set 'receives' a signal, students pick 'receivers'; in the model the receiver is the person who decodes the message, and the device is the channel.", "traps": {"a": "Receivers are the people, or audience, who get and decode the message, such as listeners and viewers; a radio or TV set carries the message to them but does not interpret it.", "b": "Senders, or sources, are the people who create and encode the message, such as the announcer or the caller; the devices only transmit what senders produce.", "c": "Feedback is the receiver's response going back to the sender, such as a reply, a nod or a phone-in call; it is a stage of the process, not a medium."}}, {"q": "Which of the following represents the cognitive component of an attitude?", "concept": "The answer is c: 'Indian food is the best of the Asian foods' represents the cognitive component of an attitude. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of a person, object, group or idea. Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) proposed the tricomponent, or ABC, model, in which every attitude has three parts. The affective component is the feeling or emotion attached to the object, such as liking, love, fear or disgust. The behavioural (conative) component is the action, or intention to act, towards it. The cognitive component is the set of beliefs, thoughts and judgements one holds about it: statements of what one thinks is true of the object. A quick test is to ask what a sentence expresses: a feeling ('I love'), a plan or action ('we are going') or a claim about the object ('it is the best'). Example: Rahul's attitude to cricket has all three parts: 'cricket is the most strategic sport' (cognitive), 'I get excited when India bats' (affective) and 'I have booked tickets for the Test match' (behavioural). Option c is right because 'Indian food is the best of the Asian foods' is a belief or judgement about the food, not a feeling or an action. Exam trap: option a sounds like an opinion, but 'I just love' expresses emotion, so it is the affective component; the cognitive component is the belief about the object's qualities.", "traps": {"a": "'I just love Indian food' expresses a feeling, so it is the affective component of the ABC model, not a belief.", "b": "'Tonight we are going to that new Indian restaurant' is an intended action, the behavioural (conative) component, not a thought about the food.", "d": "'All the above' would need all three statements to be cognitive, but a is affective and b is behavioural; only c is a belief."}}, {"q": "Treating members of various social groups differently in circumstances where their rights or treatments should be identical is:", "concept": "The answer is c: treating members of different social groups differently in situations where their rights or treatment should be identical is discrimination. Social psychologists separate three linked ideas about groups. A stereotype is a belief about the typical traits of a group's members, the cognitive part. Prejudice is a negative attitude or feeling towards people because of their group membership, mainly the affective part; Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, called it an antipathy based on a faulty and inflexible generalisation. Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair, negative or unequal action towards people because of their group, such as refusing them jobs, housing or service. Prejudice is in the mind; discrimination is in what people do. The two can come apart: a person may hold a prejudice but not act on it because of laws or norms, or may discriminate by following an unfair rule without feeling hostile. Example: Rakesh and Imran have identical degrees and interview scores, but a landlord rents the flat to Rakesh after learning that Imran belongs to a different community. Whatever the landlord feels, the unequal treatment is discrimination. Option c is right because the stem describes behaviour, treating people differently, where equal treatment is due. Exam trap: prejudice and discrimination are often used loosely as synonyms, but prejudice is the attitude and discrimination is the action; the stem's word 'treating' points to the action.", "traps": {"a": "Prejudice is the negative attitude or feeling towards a group (Gordon Allport's 'antipathy'); it may lead to unequal treatment, but the stem describes the treatment itself, which is discrimination.", "b": "Racism is prejudice and discrimination based specifically on race; the stem speaks of various social groups in general (caste, religion, gender, region as well as race), so racism is too narrow.", "d": "Dogmatism, studied by Milton Rokeach (1960), is a rigid, closed-minded certainty in one's own beliefs; it is a style of thinking, not unequal treatment of groups."}}, {"q": "The general reluctance among people in transmitting bad news to others is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the general reluctance of people to pass bad news on to others is called the MUM effect. Sidney Rosen and Abraham Tesser (1970) named it from the phrase 'keep Mum about Undesirable Messages'. In their studies, people who had to relay news to someone else passed on good news readily but delayed, softened or avoided bad news, even when they had played no part in causing it. Several reasons have been offered: the bearer does not want to feel guilty or share in the receiver's distress, fears being linked with the bad news and disliked (the 'shoot the messenger' problem), and follows a social norm against hurting others. The effect matters in workplaces and hospitals: employees hide project problems from their bosses, and doctors may delay telling a patient about a poor diagnosis. Example: Vikram's team learns that a major client has cancelled its contract. Each member waits for someone else to tell the manager, so she hears about it two days late. Option b is right because the MUM effect is the specific name for reluctance to transmit undesirable messages. Exam trap: the other options are real or plausible social psychology terms about relations between people, but none concerns passing on bad news.", "traps": {"a": "Interpersonal attraction is the tendency to like and want to be near certain people, shaped by proximity, similarity, physical attractiveness and reciprocity; it has nothing to do with withholding bad news.", "c": "Propinquity is physical nearness, which increases liking; Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) found that students in the Westgate housing project befriended the neighbours they passed most often.", "d": "'Affiliation effect' is not a standard term; affiliation is the need to be with others, which Stanley Schachter (1959) found rises when people are anxious, but it does not concern reluctance to deliver bad news."}}, {"q": "The most contributing gas to global warming and the greenhouse effect is:", "concept": "The answer is a: carbon dioxide contributes most to global warming and the human-enhanced greenhouse effect. The greenhouse effect is the warming of Earth's surface by gases that let incoming sunlight through but absorb and re-emit the heat (infrared radiation) that the Earth gives off, much as the glass of a greenhouse keeps heat in. A natural greenhouse effect keeps the planet habitable; global warming is the extra warming since the industrial revolution caused by human emissions. Carbon dioxide (CO2) comes mainly from burning coal, oil and gas and from cutting down forests. Molecule for molecule it traps less heat than methane or CFCs, but it is emitted in vastly larger amounts and much of it stays in the air for centuries, so it accounts for about two thirds of the extra heating from long-lived greenhouse gases. Environmental psychology studies the human side: why people keep high-carbon habits and how to change them. Example: Aditya works out that his daily 30 km commute by petrol car emits several kilograms of CO2; switching to the metro cuts this sharply, the kind of behaviour change environmental psychologists try to encourage. Option a is right because CO2's sheer volume and long lifetime give it the largest total warming effect of all human-emitted gases. Exam trap: methane is far more potent per molecule, so students pick b, but potency is not total contribution; water vapour, the largest natural greenhouse gas, acts as a feedback and is not among the options.", "traps": {"b": "Methane, from livestock, rice paddies, landfills and gas leaks, traps far more heat per molecule than CO2 (roughly 25 to 30 times over a century) but is present in much smaller amounts and breaks down in about a decade, so it ranks second.", "c": "Chlorofluorocarbons, once used in refrigerators and aerosol sprays, are extremely potent greenhouse gases that also destroy the ozone layer, but their tiny amounts, now falling under the 1987 Montreal Protocol, keep their total share small.", "d": "Nitrogen makes up about 78 per cent of the air but is not a greenhouse gas: its two-atom molecule does not absorb infrared heat."}}, {"q": "Carl Jung is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is b: Carl Jung is associated with analytical psychology. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), a Swiss psychiatrist, was once the favourite and chosen successor of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), but the two broke in 1913, mainly because Jung rejected Freud's view that libido is chiefly sexual energy. Jung then called his own system analytical psychology. He divided the psyche into three layers: the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten or repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious, a deeper layer inherited by all humans. The collective unconscious holds archetypes, universal patterns such as the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, denied side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in men and the masculine side in women) and the self (the archetype of wholeness). Jung also gave psychology the attitudes of introversion and extraversion and four functions: thinking, feeling, sensing and intuiting. Example: Meera dreams of a wise old woman guiding her through a forest; a Jungian analyst would read the figure as an archetype from the collective unconscious, not only as a disguised personal wish. Option b is right because analytical psychology is the name Jung gave to his school after leaving psychoanalysis. Exam trap: the neo-Freudian school names are easily swapped: individual psychology belongs to Alfred Adler (1912), psychoanalysis to Freud, and the word 'personal' appears only in Jung's personal unconscious, which is one layer of his model, not a school.", "traps": {"a": "Individual psychology is the school of Alfred Adler, who left Freud's circle in 1911 and stressed inferiority feelings, striving for superiority and style of life, not Jung's archetypes.", "c": "Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud's original system of unconscious sexual and aggressive conflict; Jung left it in 1913 and renamed his own approach.", "d": "'Personal psychology' is not a school; Jung's personal unconscious is only the individual layer of his model, beneath which lies the collective unconscious."}}, {"q": "Over secretion from thyroid glands may lead to:", "concept": "The answer is b: over-secretion of the thyroid gland tends to produce a nervous personality. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the neck. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones that set the body's metabolic rate, the speed at which cells burn fuel for energy. Too much thyroxine (hyperthyroidism, most often caused by Graves' disease, an autoimmune condition) speeds everything up: the person becomes restless, tense, irritable, anxious and excitable, sleeps poorly, has a fast heartbeat and trembling hands, feels hot and loses weight despite eating well. Too little thyroxine (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: sluggishness, poor concentration, low mood, feeling cold and weight gain in adults, and cretinism (stunted physical and mental growth) in infants if it is not treated early. Example: Kabir, a college student, becomes jittery, snaps at friends and loses six kilos in two months; a blood test shows an overactive thyroid, and his nervousness settles once treatment brings his hormone level down. This is why clinicians check thyroid function before diagnosing an anxiety disorder. Option b is right because excess thyroxine overdrives metabolism and arousal, giving the nervous, overactive picture. Exam trap: dullness and weight gain are signs of too little thyroxine, the reverse of the stem, so under-secretion signs are placed beside the correct over-secretion sign to catch students who remember only that the thyroid affects personality.", "traps": {"a": "A dull, sluggish personality comes from under-secretion (hypothyroidism), when a slow metabolism lowers energy and alertness.", "c": "Disobedience is a behaviour shaped by learning, parenting and conduct problems; it is not a recognised sign of an overactive thyroid.", "d": "Fattiness (weight gain) belongs to hypothyroidism; excess thyroxine burns fuel faster and causes weight loss."}}, {"q": "\"Personality is a dynamic organisation within an individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustment to his environment.\" This definition of personality was given by:", "concept": "The answer is d: this definition of personality was given by Gordon Allport. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, reviewed about 50 earlier definitions and proposed his own: personality is \"the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment\". Each phrase carries an idea. Dynamic organisation means personality keeps growing and changing, yet stays organised rather than being a random heap of habits. Psychophysical systems means it involves both mind and body: traits, attitudes and habits, but also nerves and glands. Determine means personality actively drives behaviour rather than just describing it. Unique adjustments stresses that no two people are alike, which fits Allport's idiographic approach, the in-depth study of the single individual. In 1961 he replaced the last phrase with \"characteristic behavior and thought\". Example: Riya and Arjun face the same job interview; Riya calms herself by rehearsing notes and Arjun by joking with the receptionist. Allport would say each shows a unique adjustment produced by a different organisation of traits. Option d is right because the wording 'dynamic organisation, psychophysical systems, unique adjustment' is Allport's classic 1937 definition, the most quoted in the field. Exam trap: Raymond Cattell (1950) also gave a famous definition, personality is \"that which permits a prediction of what a person will do in a given situation\", so students who recall only 'a trait theorist's definition' may pick Cattell.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud described personality through the id, ego and superego and unconscious conflict; he did not offer this psychophysical-systems definition.", "b": "Raymond Cattell (1950) defined personality as that which permits a prediction of what a person will do in a given situation, a prediction-based definition, not this one.", "c": "Alfred Adler described personality as a unified style of life aimed at overcoming inferiority, not as an organisation of psychophysical systems."}}, {"q": "According to Psychoanalytic theory of personality, Oedipus and Electra conflicts develop during:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Oedipus and Electra conflicts develop during the phallic stage. Sigmund Freud (1905) set out his theory of infantile sexuality in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality and completed it over the next two decades: the libido (sexual energy) centres on a different body zone at each age, giving five psychosexual stages: oral (birth to about 1, the mouth), anal (about 1 to 3, toilet training), phallic (about 3 to 6, the genitals), latency (about 6 to puberty, when sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school and friendships) and genital (puberty onward, mature sexuality). In the phallic stage the child becomes aware of the genitals, desires the opposite-sex parent and feels rivalry with the same-sex parent. In boys this is the Oedipus complex, with castration anxiety. For girls Carl Jung (1913) named the parallel the Electra complex, linked by Freud to penis envy, though Freud himself disliked the term. The conflict is resolved by identification with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego and the child's gender role. Example: four-year-old Kabir announces he will marry his mother and sulks when his father hugs her; by six he copies his father's walk and talk, a sign of identification. Option b is right because these conflicts are the defining crisis of the phallic stage. Exam trap: the genital stage sounds the most sexual and is often chosen, but it begins at puberty, when the early conflicts revive rather than first appear.", "traps": {"a": "The anal stage (about 1 to 3) centres on toilet training and control; fixation there gives anal retentive or expulsive traits, not Oedipal rivalry.", "c": "The latency stage (about 6 to puberty) follows the resolution of the Oedipus conflict, when sexual energy is dormant and redirected into learning and same-sex friendships.", "d": "The genital stage begins at puberty with mature adult sexuality; Oedipal feelings may revive then, but they first develop in the phallic stage."}}, {"q": "According to earlier views, psychological disorders stem from:", "concept": "The answer is c: according to the earliest views, psychological disorders stem from supernatural causes. This oldest explanation, called demonology or the supernatural model, blamed strange behaviour on possession by evil spirits, the anger of gods, sin or witchcraft. Treatment followed the supposed cause. Some Stone Age skulls show trephining, a hole chipped through the skull, perhaps to let an evil spirit escape. Later societies used prayer, magic and exorcism, and in late medieval Europe witch hunts were guided by the Malleus Maleficarum (1487), a manual for identifying witches. The Greek physician Hippocrates (born about 460 BC) made the first break: he argued that mental illness had natural causes in the brain and body. His humoral theory, later developed by the Greek physician Galen (second century AD), who worked in Rome, linked melancholia to an excess of black bile. Psychological causes were stressed much later, from Franz Anton Mesmer in the 1770s and the hypnotists of the Nancy school to Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895, Studies on Hysteria). Example: when Meera's grandmother in a village begins hearing voices, the family calls an exorcist instead of a doctor; that is the supernatural view still at work. Option c is right because demonology, the first known explanation of abnormality, placed the cause outside nature, in spirits and magic. Exam trap: 'natural' and 'biological' causes sound ancient because Hippocrates was ancient, but his naturalistic view replaced the even older supernatural one.", "traps": {"a": "Natural causes was the view of Hippocrates, who rejected demons and looked to the brain and body; it replaced the earlier supernatural view.", "b": "Biological causes describes Hippocrates' humoral theory and the later medical model of Emil Kraepelin (1883), not the earliest beliefs.", "d": "Psychological causes, such as unconscious conflict, were stressed only from the late 1700s and 1800s (Mesmer, the Nancy school, Freud), long after demonology."}}, {"q": "According to DSM-IV classification, Axis-I contains:", "concept": "The answer is c: in DSM-IV, Axis I contains the clinical disorders. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, published by the American Psychiatric Association, used a multiaxial system from DSM-III (1980) through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000). Each patient was described on five axes so that the clinician would look beyond the main diagnosis to the whole person. Axis I: clinical disorders, such as major depression, schizophrenia, phobias, eating disorders and substance use disorders, plus other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation (now called intellectual disability). Axis III: general medical conditions relevant to the case, such as diabetes or hypothyroidism. Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems, such as job loss, divorce or poverty. Axis V: the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a 0 to 100 rating of overall functioning. Example: Arjun's case file might read Axis I major depressive disorder; Axis II none; Axis III hypertension; Axis IV recent job loss; Axis V GAF 55. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes and merged the first three into a single list of diagnoses. Option c is right because Axis I was reserved for the clinical disorders that usually bring a person for treatment. Exam trap: the other options are the remaining axes in scrambled order, and students who remember only that personality disorders sat on a 'separate axis' may put them on Axis I instead of Axis II.", "traps": {"a": "Personality disorders (with mental retardation) were coded on Axis II, kept separate so that long-standing patterns were not overlooked behind an Axis I diagnosis.", "b": "General medical conditions, such as diabetes or a head injury, were coded on Axis III.", "d": "Psychosocial and environmental problems, such as unemployment or bereavement, were coded on Axis IV."}}, {"q": "The unpleasant and unwanted thought that keeps coming up to mind on and off in spite of a person's efforts to resist refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: an unpleasant, unwanted thought that keeps returning despite the person's efforts to resist it is an obsession. In DSM-IV and DSM-5, obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, urges or images that are felt as intrusive and unwanted and that cause marked anxiety or distress; the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Common themes are contamination, doubt ('did I lock the door?'), harm, symmetry and forbidden sexual or religious thoughts. Unlike delusions, obsessions are usually recognised as products of one's own mind. Compulsions are the repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) carried out to reduce the anxiety the obsession creates. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is the diagnosis given when obsessions, compulsions or both take up more than an hour a day or cause marked distress or impairment. Example: Meera keeps picturing germs on her hands that will make her family ill; she knows the idea is excessive but cannot shake it. That intrusive thought is the obsession; washing her hands twenty times is the compulsion; the whole pattern, if it disrupts her life, is OCD. Option c is right because the stem describes one kind of symptom, the intrusive thought itself, which is the definition of an obsession. Exam trap: OCD is tempting because it contains the word 'obsessive', but OCD names the whole disorder, while the stem asks only for the thought.", "traps": {"a": "A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation (heights, dogs, injections) that leads to avoidance, not a recurring intrusive thought.", "b": "PTSD involves intrusive memories, flashbacks and nightmares that follow a real traumatic event; the stem mentions no trauma, only a recurring unwanted thought the person resists.", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder is the full disorder made up of obsessions and/or compulsions; the stem names only the thought, which is the obsession."}}, {"q": "Raj is a fourteen year old boy and has committed theft. He will be considered as:", "concept": "The answer is c: a fourteen-year-old boy who commits theft is considered a delinquent (a juvenile delinquent). Juvenile delinquency means an act by a minor that would be a crime if an adult did it, such as theft, assault or vandalism. The law treats young offenders separately because they are still developing and seen as open to reform. In India, the Juvenile Justice Act of 1986 set the limit at under 16 for boys and under 18 for girls; the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2000 made it under 18 for everyone, and the 2015 Act calls such a person a 'child in conflict with law', whose case goes to a Juvenile Justice Board rather than an ordinary criminal court. The aim is rehabilitation (counselling, observation homes, probation), not punishment. In clinical terms, a repeated pattern of serious rule breaking in a child may be diagnosed as conduct disorder, but delinquency itself is a legal and social label. Example: Raj's case would be heard by the Juvenile Justice Board, which might place him under the supervision of a probation officer while he continues school. Option c is right because Raj is a minor, so his theft is handled as delinquency. Exam trap: 'psychopath' and 'sociopath' sound like explanations for antisocial acts, but they are personality labels for a lasting callous pattern, and antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before 18; a single theft at 14 makes Raj a delinquent, not a psychopath.", "traps": {"a": "Sociopath is an older label for antisocial personality, a persistent pattern of disregard for others' rights that stresses social causes; it is not given for a single act at 14.", "b": "Psychopath describes a lasting pattern of callousness, lack of guilt and manipulation (Hervey Cleckley, 1941; Robert Hare's Psychopathy Checklist); one theft does not establish it, and such adult labels are not used for a 14-year-old.", "d": "Criminal is the legal term for an adult convicted of an offence by a court; a 14-year-old is a minor dealt with under juvenile justice law."}}, {"q": "Who is considered as father of experimental psychology?", "concept": "The answer is d: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology (the option spells his first name the English way, William). Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician and physiologist, argued that conscious experience could be studied with the controlled methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) mapped out the new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig he and his students began formal experiments in what is taken as the first psychology laboratory. For this reason 1879 is treated as the birth year of psychology as an independent science. He also founded the first journal for experimental psychology, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained many of the first generation of psychologists from Europe and America. His method was experimental introspection: trained observers responded to precisely controlled stimuli (clicks, flashes of light, metronome beats) and reported their immediate experience while their reaction times were measured. Example: in a Wundt-style practical, Kabir presses a key the instant he hears a click, then again only after he has clearly recognised the sound; the extra time estimates how long recognition takes. Option d is right because Wundt gave psychology its first formal laboratory, its first experimental journal and its first trained experimenters. Exam trap: each other name is the 'father' of something else: Sigmund Freud (1900) of psychoanalysis, John B. Watson (1913) of behaviourism and William James (1890) of American psychology; Gustav Fechner (1860) is sometimes also credited, as the founder of psychophysics.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud is the father of psychoanalysis; he built his theory from clinical case studies, free association and dream analysis, not laboratory experiments.", "b": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism with his 1913 paper 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', more than thirty years after Wundt's laboratory opened.", "c": "William James is the father of American psychology and functionalism (The Principles of Psychology, 1890); his small Harvard laboratory was used mainly for teaching demonstrations."}}, {"q": "In a controlled situation, the group of subjects was exposed to all experimental conditions or variables except to the independent variable. This group of subjects is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the group exposed to every condition of the experiment except the independent variable is the control group. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable (IV), the presumed cause, and measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV), the outcome. Participants who receive the IV (the treatment) form the experimental group. Participants who are treated in exactly the same way, with the same room, instructions, timing and measurements, but who do not receive the IV, form the control group. Because the two groups differ only in the IV, any difference in the DV can be put down to it. The control group therefore provides a baseline: it shows what would have happened anyway. Random assignment, in which chance decides who goes into each group, makes the groups equal at the start. In drug trials the control group often receives a placebo, an inactive pill, so that expectations are equal as well. Example: Riya tests whether background music improves memory; one group of students learns a word list with music playing (experimental group) and another learns the same list in the same room in silence (control group). Option c is right because the stem describes exactly the group that receives everything except the IV. Exam trap: the other options borrow words from the language of variables ('independent', 'dependent', 'extraneous') and turn them into made-up group names, which misleads students who half remember the vocabulary.", "traps": {"a": "'Independent group' is not a group name; independent refers to the variable the experimenter manipulates, or to an independent-groups design in which different people serve in each condition.", "b": "'Dependent group' is not a standard term; the dependent variable is the outcome measured in both the experimental and the control group.", "d": "'Extraneous group' does not exist; extraneous variables are unwanted influences other than the IV, which a control group helps to rule out."}}, {"q": "The minimum amount of energy that can be detected 50 percent of the time is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the minimum amount of stimulus energy that can be detected 50 per cent of the time is the absolute threshold. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, founded psychophysics, the measurement of the link between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. He found that there is no sharp line between 'not sensed' and 'sensed': as a stimulus grows stronger, the chance of detecting it rises gradually. So the absolute threshold is defined statistically, as the intensity detected on half of the trials. Classic everyday estimates (Eugene Galanter, 1962) are striking: on a clear, dark night a candle flame can be seen about 30 miles away, and a watch ticking can be heard about 20 feet away in a quiet room. Fechner's methods for finding thresholds are the method of limits, the method of constant stimuli and the method of adjustment (average error). Example: Arjun sits in a dark room while a dim light is flashed many times at different strengths; the strength he reports seeing on half the trials is his absolute threshold for light. Option a is right because 'minimum energy detected 50 per cent of the time' is the textbook definition of the absolute threshold. Exam trap: the difference threshold (just noticeable difference) uses the same 50 per cent rule, but it is the smallest change between two stimuli that can be noticed, not the weakest stimulus that can be detected at all.", "traps": {"b": "The difference threshold (just noticeable difference) is the smallest change between two stimuli detected 50 per cent of the time; Ernst Weber (1834) showed it is a constant fraction of the original stimulus.", "c": "The response criterion comes from signal detection theory (Wilson Tanner and John Swets, 1954; book by David Green and John Swets, 1966): it is the person's willingness to say 'yes', shaped by expectations and motives, not an amount of energy.", "d": "Signal detection theory replaces the idea of a fixed threshold, holding that detection depends on both sensitivity and the response criterion; it is a theory, not a minimum stimulus level."}}, {"q": "Variable that might have affected the dependent variable along with or instead of the independent variable is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a variable that may have affected the dependent variable along with, or instead of, the independent variable is a confounding variable. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV). Any other variable that could influence the DV is an extraneous variable. An extraneous variable becomes a confounding variable when it changes systematically together with the IV, so its effect is mixed up (confounded) with the IV's effect. The researcher then cannot tell whether the IV, the confound or both produced the result, and the study loses internal validity, its ability to show cause and effect. Common confounds include time of day, experimenter expectations, pre-existing differences between groups and order effects. They are controlled by random assignment, holding conditions constant, counterbalancing and blinding. Example: Arjun tests a new teaching method on a morning class and the old method on an afternoon class. The morning class scores higher, but those students may simply be fresher, so time of day is confounded with teaching method and the result proves nothing. Option b is right because the stem describes a variable whose effect cannot be separated from, and may even replace, the effect of the IV. Exam trap: 'control variable' sounds close, but a control variable is one the researcher deliberately holds constant precisely so that it cannot become a confound.", "traps": {"a": "The dependent variable is the outcome being measured (scores, reaction times); it is what the variables act on, not a rival cause acting on it.", "c": "A control variable is an extraneous variable that the researcher deliberately holds constant across conditions, which is how confounds are prevented.", "d": "The independent variable is the factor the researcher manipulates; the stem describes something acting along with or instead of it."}}, {"q": "When a person perceives an incomplete triangle as a complete one, he is following ______ principle of perception.", "concept": "The answer is a: perceiving an incomplete triangle as a complete one follows the principle of closure. The Gestalt psychologists, Max Wertheimer (1923), Kurt Koffka (1935) and Wolfgang Kohler (1929), argued that perception is organised into meaningful wholes and that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. Wertheimer described laws of perceptual organisation by which the brain groups separate elements. Closure is the tendency to fill in gaps and see an incomplete figure as complete and closed. The other grouping laws include proximity (nearby items are grouped), similarity (items that look alike are grouped) and continuity (we follow smooth, continuous lines and curves). Figure-ground organisation, seeing an object stand out from its background, is even more basic. All of these serve the broader law of Pragnanz, the tendency to see the simplest, most stable form available. Example: Riya glances at a logo made of three black corners with gaps between them, yet she immediately sees a triangle; her perception has closed the gaps. Closure is also why she can read a word on the blackboard that is partly rubbed out. Option a is right because filling in the missing sides to see a whole triangle is the definition of closure. Exam trap: continuity is tempting because the eye seems to 'continue' the lines across the gaps, but continuity is about following an unbroken line or curve, while closure is about completing a figure into a whole.", "traps": {"b": "Contrast is a stimulus property that draws attention: a stimulus that differs sharply in size, colour or intensity from its surroundings stands out; it does not complete missing parts.", "c": "Figure and ground (Edgar Rubin's vase-faces figure, 1915) is seeing an object stand out against its background; it does not fill in gaps in an outline.", "d": "Similarity groups items that look alike, and continuity makes us follow smooth unbroken lines; neither explains turning an incomplete outline into a whole triangle."}}, {"q": "Linear perspective and interposition are examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: linear perspective and interposition are examples of monocular cues. Depth cues are pieces of information that let us judge distance and see a three-dimensional world from the flat images on our retinas. Monocular cues work even with one eye closed; most are also called pictorial cues because artists use them to suggest depth on a flat canvas. In linear perspective, parallel lines such as railway tracks seem to converge as they recede into the distance. In interposition (overlap), an object that blocks part of another is seen as nearer. Other monocular cues are relative size, texture gradient (texture looks finer with distance), aerial perspective (distant hills look hazy and bluish), light and shadow, height in the visual field and motion parallax (when we move, near objects seem to sweep past faster than far ones). Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different image, which the brain fuses into depth) and convergence (the eyes turn inward more for near objects). Example: Kabir covers one eye and looks down a long road lined with trees; the road narrows towards the horizon and nearer trees hide parts of farther ones, so he still sees depth. Option b is right because both cues work with a single eye and can be shown in a painting. Exam trap: options c and d are not separate categories but the two binocular cues themselves, placed to catch students who link every depth question to disparity and convergence.", "traps": {"a": "Binocular cues need both eyes working together (retinal disparity and convergence); linear perspective and interposition work with one eye alone.", "c": "Binocular (retinal) disparity is a binocular cue: the slight difference between the two eyes' images, which the brain uses for stereoscopic depth.", "d": "Convergence is a binocular cue: the inward turning of both eyes, sensed through the eye muscles, which increases for near objects."}}, {"q": "Vernier chronoscope is an instrument used to measure:", "concept": "The answer is c: the vernier chronoscope is an instrument used to measure reaction time. A chronoscope is any device for timing very short intervals, and in early psychology laboratories its main job was timing reaction time, the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the person's response. The best known was the Hipp chronoscope, built by the clockmaker Matthaus Hipp in the 1840s, a clockwork timer read in thousandths of a second that Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used in his Leipzig laboratory. Reaction time mattered because Franciscus Donders (1868) showed that subtracting a simple reaction time from a more complex one estimates how long a mental process takes, a method called mental chronometry. The vernier chronoscope, described by the American psychologist Edmund Sanford (1898), is a cheaper pendulum version. The stimulus releases one pendulum and the response releases a second, slightly faster one; counting the swings until the faster pendulum catches up gives the interval, read in the same way as the vernier scale on a calliper. Example: in a practical class, Meera presses a key as soon as a bell rings, and the chronoscope shows her simple reaction time, a fraction of a second. Option c is right because the chronoscope measures time, and reaction time is what psychology laboratories time with it. Exam trap: each other process has its own classic apparatus: the tachistoscope for brief exposures in perception and attention, the aesthesiometer for touch sensation and the memory drum for memory.", "traps": {"a": "Perception studies classically used the tachistoscope, which flashes a display for a controlled fraction of a second; the chronoscope only times intervals.", "b": "Sensation is studied with psychophysical methods and devices such as the aesthesiometer (two-point touch threshold) or the audiometer (hearing), not with a timer.", "d": "Memory was studied with the memory drum, which shows items one at a time at a fixed rate, following Hermann Ebbinghaus's (1885) work with nonsense syllables."}}, {"q": "Most of the experimental work on Span of Attention was conducted by:", "concept": "The answer is a: most of the early experimental work on the span of attention was done by J. M. Cattell, that is, James McKeen Cattell. The span of attention (span of apprehension) is the number of separate items a person can grasp in a single brief glance, without counting. The question was first tried by William Stanley Jevons (1871), who threw handfuls of beans into a box and judged their number at a glance: he was never wrong with three or four. James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944), an American who took his doctorate with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig in 1886, studied the span systematically with the tachistoscope, a device that flashes a display for a tiny fraction of a second so that the eyes cannot move or count. He found that the span is small for unrelated letters but far larger when the letters form familiar words, and larger again when the words make a sentence, because meaningful material is grasped in bigger units. Later tachistoscope studies found that an adult grasps only about four to eight separate items in one glance, fewer for unrelated letters than for simple dots. Example: Arjun sees a card of random dots flashed for a tenth of a second and reports five correctly, but when the card shows a short familiar word he reads every letter. Option a is right because Cattell's tachistoscope experiments are the classic body of work on the span of attention. Exam trap: George Miller's (1956) 'seven, plus or minus two' is the span of short-term memory, a different process, and Hermann Ebbinghaus is a tempting pick because he too ran early experiments, but on memory.", "traps": {"b": "Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) studied memory and forgetting with nonsense syllables, giving the forgetting curve and the savings method, not the span of attention.", "c": "Max Wertheimer launched Gestalt psychology with his study of apparent movement, the phi phenomenon (1912); his work was on perceptual organisation, not attention span.", "d": "Gordon Allport (1937) is known for trait theory and his definition of personality, not for experiments on attention."}}, {"q": "A researcher has obtained the data on the basis of gender and religion. This type of data is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is b: data obtained on the basis of gender and religion form a discrete series. In statistics a series is a set of observations arranged for analysis. A discrete (discontinuous) series has separate, distinct values with nothing possible in between; such data are counted in whole numbers. A continuous series comes from a variable that can take any value within a range, including fractions, so it is measured rather than counted and is usually grouped into class intervals such as 150 to 155 cm. Gender and religion are categories: each person falls into exactly one class (male or female; Hindu, Muslim, Sikh, Christian), and the researcher can only count how many people fall into each. There is no value halfway between Sikh and Christian, and there cannot be 20.5 Hindus. Height, weight, age and reaction time, by contrast, are continuous. Example: Riya surveys 60 students and records 34 Hindus, 15 Muslims and 11 Sikhs; these frequencies are discrete counts, whereas the same students' heights in centimetres would form a continuous series. Option b is right because category data can only be counted in separate whole units, which is the mark of a discrete series. Exam trap: 'Both (a) and (b)' looks safe, but a variable with no in-between values at all cannot also be continuous. Key note: strictly, gender and religion are nominal (categorical) variables, which some texts call qualitative attributes rather than a numerical series; since nominal data are always discrete, discrete series is the best fit among the options, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "A continuous series holds measurements that can take any value in a range, such as height or reaction time; gender and religion have no in-between values.", "c": "'Both' fails because these categories cannot take fractional or intermediate values, so they are discrete only, not continuous as well.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because discrete series (option b) fits: category data are counted in separate whole units."}}, {"q": "The mean and median of a given data is 7 and 12 respectively. What will be its mode?", "concept": "The answer is d: when the mean is 7 and the median is 12, the mode is 22. For a moderately skewed distribution, Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) noted an empirical relation among the three averages: the median lies about one third of the way from the mean to the mode, which gives Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. Substituting the values: Mode = 3(12) minus 2(7) = 36 minus 14 = 22. The rule is called empirical because it was found by observing real distributions rather than derived as an exact law, so it holds only approximately and only for moderately skewed data. In a perfectly symmetrical (normal) distribution the mean, median and mode coincide. When they separate, their order shows the direction of skew: here the mean (7) is below the median (12), which is below the mode (22), so the long tail lies to the left and the distribution is negatively skewed. A few very low scores drag the mean down most, the median less and the mode not at all. Example: on an easy class test, most of Kabir's classmates score high, but a handful who barely attempted it score near zero, pulling the mean well below the median and mode. Option d is right because 3 times 12 minus 2 times 7 equals 22. Exam trap: the wrong options come from simpler operations on the same two numbers: 19 is mean plus median, 5 is median minus mean and 9.5 is their average, so a student who forgets the weights 3 and 2 lands on one of them.", "traps": {"a": "19 is simply mean plus median (7 + 12); the empirical formula weights the median by 3 and subtracts twice the mean.", "b": "5 is median minus mean (12 minus 7), the gap between them, not the mode.", "c": "9.5 is the average of the mean and median; in a skewed distribution the mode lies beyond the median, on the far side from the mean, not between them."}}, {"q": "Which of the following graphical representation needs two extra intervals or classes?", "concept": "The answer is b: the frequency polygon needs two extra intervals or classes. A frequency distribution groups scores into class intervals (for example 20 to 24, 25 to 29) and records how many cases fall in each. To draw a frequency polygon, the midpoint of each interval is marked on the X axis, a point is plotted above it at the height of its frequency, and the points are joined by straight lines. If the line stopped at the first and last intervals, the figure would hang in the air above the baseline. So one extra interval with zero frequency is added below the lowest class and one above the highest, and the line is carried down to the baseline at their midpoints. This closes the figure and makes the area under the polygon equal to the area of the histogram for the same data, as Henry Garrett (1926) explains in Statistics in Psychology and Education. Polygons are especially useful for comparing two distributions on one graph. Example: Meera's test scores run from 20 to 49 in intervals of 5; she adds empty intervals of 15 to 19 and 50 to 54 so that her polygon starts and ends on the X axis. Option b is right because adding an empty class at each end is a defining step in drawing a frequency polygon. Exam trap: the histogram is drawn from the same table, but its bars stand on the exact class limits and need no extra classes. Key note: a smoothed frequency curve is often drawn from the closed polygon, so it can seem to use the extra intervals too, but the step of adding an empty interval at each end belongs to the frequency polygon itself, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "A histogram uses adjoining bars standing on the exact limits of each class interval; it rests on the baseline by itself and needs no extra classes.", "c": "A frequency curve is a smoothed version of the polygon drawn freehand through the points; adding the two empty intervals is the polygon's own step.", "d": "A bar chart shows separate bars with gaps for categorical data such as religion or gender; it has no class intervals to extend."}}, {"q": "If the distribution is normal or near normal and the researcher is in need of further statistics from the measure of dispersion, then the most appropriate variability measure is:", "concept": "The answer is d: when the distribution is normal or near normal and further statistics are needed, the most appropriate measure of variability is the standard deviation. Variability (dispersion) describes how spread out scores are around the average. The standard deviation (SD, sigma) is the square root of the mean of the squared deviations of scores from the mean: SD = square root of (sum of x squared divided by N), where x is each score minus the mean. Karl Pearson named it in lectures in 1893. Because it uses every score and squares the deviations instead of dropping their signs, it can be handled algebraically, and it is the most stable measure of spread from sample to sample. It is tied to the normal curve: about 68 per cent of cases fall within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. It is also the building block of further statistics: z scores and other standard scores, correlation, the t test, analysis of variance and reliability coefficients. Example: Arjun's and Riya's classes both average 60 marks, but Arjun's SD is 4 and Riya's is 15, so Riya's class is far more mixed; with the SD each mark can also be turned into a z score. Option d is right because the SD is the most reliable measure listed and the only one that feeds into higher statistics. Exam trap: quartile deviation is the right choice for the opposite case, skewed or open-ended data, where it partners the median; normal data go with the SD, skewed data with the quartile deviation.", "traps": {"a": "The range (highest minus lowest score) is the crudest measure: it rests on the two extreme scores and is unstable, so it suits only a quick rough look.", "b": "The average (mean) deviation ignores plus and minus signs, so it cannot be used in further algebraic work, even though it uses every score.", "c": "The quartile deviation, half the interquartile range, goes with the median and suits skewed or open-ended distributions, not normal data needing further statistics."}}, {"q": "The researcher has selected those participants who are interested to participate and will provide the richest information. He is using ______ sampling technique.", "concept": "The answer is c: the researcher is using purposive sampling, because he deliberately chose the people best placed to give rich information. Purposive (judgemental) sampling is a non-probability method: the researcher uses his own judgement about the aims of the study to hand-pick participants, instead of leaving selection to chance. Michael Quinn Patton (1990) described its logic as selecting 'information-rich cases', people from whom one can learn a great deal about the issues central to the research. It suits qualitative work, where depth matters more than generalisation, but the sample may not represent the population. In probability sampling, by contrast, every member of the population has a known chance of selection: simple random sampling gives everyone an equal chance; stratified random sampling divides the population into strata (such as gender or class) and draws randomly within each; cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups (schools, villages) and studies everyone in them. Example: Meera is studying how first-generation college students cope with English-medium classes. Instead of drawing names from the register, she approaches willing first-generation students who can describe their experience in detail. Option c is right because the choice was made deliberately, for the purpose of the study and the richness of information, not by chance. Exam trap: 'interested to participate' sounds like volunteer (self-selected) sampling, which is not offered; the deciding phrase is 'will provide the richest information', the hallmark of purposive sampling.", "traps": {"a": "Random sampling is a probability method in which every member of the population has an equal chance of selection, as in a lottery; here the researcher chose people deliberately, so chance played no part.", "b": "Stratified random sampling divides the population into subgroups (strata) and draws randomly within each; it still relies on chance, not on the researcher's judgement of who will give the richest information.", "d": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups, such as schools or villages, and studies all their members; it is a probability method used to cover large areas cheaply, not a way of picking information-rich individuals."}}, {"q": "The perfect negative correlation is represented by:", "concept": "The answer is c: a perfect negative correlation is written as -1 (strictly -1.00). A correlation coefficient describes the direction and strength of the linear relationship between two variables. Francis Galton (1888) described such 'co-relations', and Karl Pearson (1896) gave them an exact formula, the product-moment coefficient r. The coefficient always lies between -1.00 and +1.00. The sign shows the direction: a positive sign means both variables rise together; a negative sign means that as one rises, the other falls. The size shows the strength: 0 means no linear relationship, and the closer the value is to 1 (either sign), the stronger the relationship. In a perfect correlation every point on the scatter plot falls exactly on a straight line, so one variable can be predicted from the other without error; when that line slopes downwards, r = -1. Example: Arjun starts the month with 600 rupees and spends exactly 20 rupees every day. The number of days passed and the money left correlate at exactly -1, because each extra day removes the same amount. Real psychological data almost never reach -1 or +1. Option c is right because -1 is the lower limit of the coefficient and marks a perfect inverse relationship. Exam trap: -100 and +100 look like percentages, but a correlation is not a percentage and can never exceed 1 in size. Also, a negative sign does not mean weak: r = -0.90 is a stronger relationship than r = +0.40.", "traps": {"a": "-100 is impossible: correlation coefficients cannot go beyond -1.00 or +1.00, so -100 mistakes the coefficient for a percentage.", "b": "+100 is also outside the possible range; even a perfect positive relationship is written +1, never +100.", "d": "+1 is a perfect positive correlation, in which both variables rise together exactly in step; the stem asks for the perfect negative one, -1."}}, {"q": "The critical values of \"t\" (in large sample) at 0.05 significance level are:", "concept": "The answer is b: in large samples the critical values of t at the 0.05 level are 1.96 (two-tailed) and 1.64 (one-tailed). The t distribution was published by William Sealy Gosset (1908), writing as 'Student' from the Guinness brewery in Dublin. It is bell-shaped like the normal curve but has heavier tails, so with few degrees of freedom (df) its critical values are larger: at df = 10 the two-tailed 0.05 value is 2.23. As df grow, the t distribution closes in on the normal curve, and with large samples (df above about 30, and certainly beyond 120) its critical values become the familiar z values. A critical value is the cut-off t must reach to reject the null hypothesis. At the 0.05 level, a two-tailed (non-directional) test splits the 5 per cent between both tails, 2.5 per cent each, giving 1.96; a one-tailed (directional) test puts the whole 5 per cent in one tail, giving 1.645, printed as 1.64 or 1.65. Example: Riya compares the reaction times of 200 athletes and 200 non-athletes. Her t of 2.10 passes 1.96, so the difference is significant at 0.05 two-tailed, but it falls short of 2.58, so it is not significant at 0.01. Option b is right because 1.96 and 1.64 are the two-tailed and one-tailed cut-offs at the 0.05 level. Exam trap: the four numbers are easy to swap. Learn them as pairs: 1.96 and 2.58 are two-tailed (0.05, 0.01); 1.64 and 2.33 are one-tailed. Key note: the paper prints 1.64, which is 1.645 rounded down; some texts round it to 1.65 instead, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "1.96 is right for 0.05 two-tailed, but 2.33 is the one-tailed value at the stricter 0.01 level, so this pair mixes two significance levels.", "c": "2.58 and 2.33 are both 0.01 values (two-tailed and one-tailed); they belong to the 1 per cent level, not the 5 per cent level the stem asks about.", "d": "2.58 and 1.96 are the two-tailed values at 0.01 and 0.05 respectively, so this pair mixes levels instead of giving the two-tailed and one-tailed values at 0.05."}}, {"q": "Measurement is generally ______ whereas Evaluation is ______.", "concept": "The answer is a: measurement is generally quantitative, whereas evaluation is qualitative, because evaluation adds a value judgement to the numbers. Measurement is the assignment of numbers to objects or events according to rules, as Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) defined it. It answers the question 'how much?': a score of 42 out of 60, or a height of 120 cm. Measurement only describes. Evaluation is the wider process of judging worth or desirability. It asks 'how good?' or 'how far have the objectives been met?', and it uses test scores plus unmeasured information such as observation, interviews and anecdotal records. Norman E. Gronlund, whose Measurement and Evaluation in Teaching first appeared in 1965, summed this up in a well-known formula: evaluation equals quantitative description (measurement) and/or qualitative description (non-measurement), plus value judgements. So measurement is one narrower, more objective part of evaluation. Example: Riya scores 42 out of 60 in mathematics; that is measurement. Her teacher then writes, 'Riya has done well, has mastered fractions, needs more practice with word problems and is ready for the next unit'; that is evaluation. Option a is right because measurement yields numbers (quantitative) and evaluation interprets them with judgements of worth (qualitative). Exam trap: option b simply reverses the pair, and option c reverses it again in other words, since objectivity belongs mainly to measurement and subjective judgement to evaluation. Key note: evaluation also uses quantitative data, so it is not purely qualitative, but the standard contrast in Indian measurement texts is 'measurement is quantitative, evaluation is qualitative (judgemental)', so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the contrast: measurement produces numbers (quantitative), and evaluation adds judgement (qualitative), not the other way round.", "c": "This is also reversed: measurement aims to be objective (rule-based numbers that any scorer would obtain), while evaluation involves subjective judgements of worth.", "d": "True and false describe statements or a type of test item (true-false items), not the nature of measurement and evaluation; neither process is 'false'."}}, {"q": "The first step in the construction of a psychological test is:", "concept": "The answer is b: planning is the first step in constructing a psychological test. The steps run in a fixed order. (1) Planning: the constructor fixes the purpose of the test, defines the trait or content to be measured, decides the target group, the type of items, the number of items, the time limit and the scoring method, and draws up a blueprint or table of specifications showing how many items will cover each area. (2) Item writing: far more items are written than will be kept. (3) Preliminary try-out: the draft is given to a sample like the target group. (4) Item analysis: each item's difficulty and discrimination are worked out, and weak items are revised or dropped. (5) Reliability is established. (6) Validity is established. (7) Norms are developed on a large, representative standardisation sample. (8) A manual is written describing administration, scoring and interpretation. Some Indian texts count seven steps, but all begin with planning. Example: Arjun wants to build an examination anxiety scale in Hindi for Class 10 students. Before writing a single statement, he must decide which aspects to cover (worry, bodily tension, avoidance), how many items each gets and the response format. Option b is right because every later step, from item writing to norms, follows from the decisions made at the planning stage. Exam trap: item writing feels like the obvious start because it is the first visible product, but it comes second; norms come near the end, after reliability and validity.", "traps": {"a": "Writing items is the second step; items are written to fit the blueprint, so they cannot come before planning has fixed the content, format and number of items.", "c": "Preliminary administration (the try-out) is the third step, given only after a draft pool of items exists, so that item analysis can be carried out on the responses.", "d": "Developing norms is one of the last steps, done on a large standardisation sample after the final form has been shown to be reliable and valid."}}, {"q": "The relationship between reliability and validity of a test is best represented by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the highest validity a test can reach is the square root of the product of the test's reliability and the criterion's reliability, r12max = √(r11 × r22). Here r12 is the validity coefficient (the correlation between the test, variable 1, and a criterion, variable 2), r11 is the reliability of the test and r22 the reliability of the criterion. Every obtained score is true score plus random error, and random error correlates with nothing, so only the true-score part of a test can correlate with anything outside it. The more error either measure carries, the lower the ceiling. If the criterion is perfectly reliable (r22 = 1), the formula shrinks to √r11, the index of reliability, which is the correlation between obtained and true scores. Run in reverse, the same reasoning gives the correction for attenuation of Charles Spearman (1904). Example: Kabir's aptitude test has a reliability of 0.81, and the supervisor ratings he uses as the criterion have a reliability of 0.64. The highest validity possible is √(0.81 × 0.64) = √0.5184 = 0.72; a reported validity of 0.85 would be impossible. Option b is right because it is the only formula that sets the validity ceiling from the two reliabilities. Exam trap: the rule runs one way only. A test can be reliable without being valid (a consistent measure of the wrong thing), but it cannot be valid without being reliable: reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity.", "traps": {"a": "This puts the validity coefficient r12 inside its own ceiling and leaves out the criterion's reliability r22; the ceiling is set by the two reliabilities, r11 and r22.", "c": "This writes a maximum for reliability (r11) as a sum of square roots, which is not a formula of classical test theory; the two reliabilities are multiplied under one root, and the quantity capped is validity.", "d": "This puts the criterion's reliability r22 on the left and the validity r12 under the root, mixing up the terms; the quantity being capped is validity, r12, and the root holds r11 and r22."}}, {"q": "The assessment of simultaneous relationship between learning disability test and school performance test is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: correlating a learning disability test with school performance measured at the same time is an example of concurrent validity. In criterion-related validity, test scores are correlated with an outside measure of the same quality, called the criterion. Its two forms differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time, so the question is whether the test agrees with the person's present standing; it is often used to check a new test against an established measure or a current diagnosis. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later, so the question is whether the test forecasts future performance, as when an entrance test is correlated with first-year marks. The American Psychological Association's Technical Recommendations (1954) listed concurrent and predictive validity separately; later Standards grouped them together as criterion-related validity. Example: Meera, a school psychologist, gives her new learning disability screening test to 100 Class 4 children in the same week that their teachers record current marks in reading and arithmetic. If low test scores go with low current marks, the test shows concurrent validity. Option c is right because 'simultaneous' means the criterion is gathered at the same time as the test. Exam trap: predictive and concurrent validity use the same correlation, and the only difference is when the criterion is measured. Watch for 'simultaneous' or 'current' (concurrent) against 'later' or 'future' (predictive).", "traps": {"a": "Face validity is only whether a test looks, to test takers and lay people, as if it measures what it claims; it involves no correlation with a criterion such as school performance.", "b": "Predictive validity also correlates a test with a criterion, but the criterion is measured later (for example, next year's marks); here the two are measured simultaneously.", "d": "Content validity is judged by experts checking that the items adequately sample the whole domain, often against a blueprint; it does not compare the test with another measure."}}, {"q": "When the internal consistency is the main source of error, then the most appropriate reliability method is:", "concept": "The answer is b: when inconsistency among the items is the main source of error, the split-half method is the most appropriate of these. Reliability is consistency of measurement, and each method of estimating it targets a different source of error. Error from time (mood or memory changing between occasions) calls for test-retest reliability, the same test given twice. Error from the particular set of items chosen calls for alternate (parallel) forms. Error from items within one test not measuring the same thing calls for internal consistency methods, which need one administration. In the split-half method the test is divided into two comparable halves, usually odd and even items, and the two half-scores are correlated. Because this gives the reliability of a test only half as long, the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) steps it up: full reliability = 2r / (1 + r). Other internal consistency methods are the Kuder-Richardson formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937) and Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951). Example: Kabir gives his 40-item self-esteem scale once to 300 students, scores the odd and even items separately and finds that the halves correlate at 0.70; Spearman-Brown gives 2(0.70) / 1.70 = 0.82. Option b is right because split-half is the only internal consistency method among the options. Exam trap: kappa sounds technical, but Cohen's kappa measures agreement between raters, a different source of error (the scorer, not the items).", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice and correlates the scores; it estimates stability over time, so it targets error from changes between occasions, not inconsistency among items.", "c": "Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) measures agreement between two raters beyond chance; it addresses scorer error (inter-rater reliability), not the internal consistency of a test's items.", "d": "Alternate or parallel forms reliability correlates two equivalent versions of a test; it estimates equivalence across forms and needs two tests, rather than checking consistency among the items of one test."}}, {"q": "The Behaviouristic approach of personality mostly relies on:", "concept": "The answer is b: the behaviouristic approach to personality relies mostly on objective techniques. Behaviourists such as John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1953) treat personality as a set of learned responses shaped by the environment, not as hidden traits or unconscious drives, so they assess what a person actually does in observable, countable terms. Walter Mischel (1968) strengthened this view by showing that trait scores predict behaviour across situations only weakly. Behavioural assessment defines the target behaviour operationally ('hits a classmate'), then measures it directly: naturalistic or analogue observation, frequency and duration counts, behavioural checklists, self-monitoring diaries and ABC records of antecedent, behaviour and consequence. Subjective techniques (autobiography, introspective self-description, unstructured interviews) depend on the person's view of himself, and projective techniques (Rorschach, TAT) assume hidden motives show through responses to ambiguous material; humanistic and psychodynamic psychologists favour these. Example: to assess Arjun's classroom aggression, a behavioural psychologist does not show him inkblots. She watches him for a week, records each push or shout, notes what came just before (being teased) and just after (classmates laughing) and counts incidents per day. Option b is right because behaviourists measure observable behaviour objectively. Exam trap: projective tests carry the prestige of clinical psychology, but they belong to the psychodynamic approach, not to behaviourism. Key note: some texts call self-report inventories such as the MMPI and 16PF 'objective tests', although they are self-reports; the point of the item is that behaviourists rely on objective measurement of observable behaviour, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Subjective techniques, such as autobiography, introspective self-reports and unstructured interviews, rest on the person's own view of himself; behaviourists distrust them because inner reports cannot be directly observed or checked.", "c": "Projective techniques (Rorschach, TAT, sentence completion) come from the psychodynamic approach and assume unconscious motives are projected onto ambiguous stimuli; behaviourists reject such inferred inner causes.", "d": "All of the above fails because behaviourists do not rely on subjective or projective techniques; only objective techniques fit their emphasis on observable behaviour."}}, {"q": "The term projective technique was coined by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the term 'projective technique' (projective methods) was coined by Lawrence K. Frank (1939). In his paper 'Projective methods for the study of personality' in the Journal of Psychology, Frank grouped together tests in which a person meets an ambiguous, loosely structured stimulus and is free to respond in any way. Because the material has no fixed meaning, the person projects his own needs, conflicts, feelings and ways of seeing the world into the response. Frank likened these methods to an X-ray, which shows inner structure without opening the body. In psychoanalysis, projection was a defence: attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to someone else. The two best-known tests existed before Frank named the group: the inkblot test of Hermann Rorschach (1921), published in Psychodiagnostik, and the Thematic Apperception Test (1935) of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray. Example: shown a TAT picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced by his parents to practise. The theme of pressure comes from her, not from the picture, and that is what projective methods try to capture. Option d is right because Frank gave the whole family of tests its name. Exam trap: Hermann Rorschach created the most famous projective test, so he is the tempting answer, but creating a test is not coining the term; Rorschach himself presented his work as a diagnostic experiment in perception.", "traps": {"a": "Francis Galton pioneered word association (1879), an ancestor of verbal projective methods, along with fingerprinting and early mental tests, but he did not coin the term 'projective technique'.", "b": "Hermann Rorschach created the inkblot test (Psychodiagnostik, 1921), the best-known projective test, but the general term 'projective methods' was introduced later by Lawrence K. Frank (1939).", "c": "Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray created the Thematic Apperception Test (1935), another classic projective test, but they did not name the category."}}, {"q": "In the Rorschach Ink Blot Test (RIBT), Human (H), Animal (A) and Nature (N) are the types of:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach Inkblot Test, Human (H), Animal (A) and Nature (N) are content categories. Hermann Rorschach (1921) devised ten symmetrical inkblots, five in black and grey and five with colour. The person says what each blot might be, then explains where and why in the inquiry. Each response is scored on several dimensions, worked out by Rorschach and refined by Samuel Beck (1944), Bruno Klopfer (1942) and John Exner, whose Comprehensive System appeared in 1974. Location asks where the percept was seen: W (whole blot), D (common detail), Dd (unusual detail), S (white space). Determinants ask which feature of the blot made it look that way: F (form), M (human movement), C (colour), shading and texture. Content asks what was seen: H (human), Hd (human detail), A (animal), Ad (animal detail), An (anatomy), N or Na (nature, such as landscapes), Sx (sex) and so on. Form quality asks how well the percept fits the shape of the blot (F+ good, F- poor). Example: on Card V Kabir says, 'The whole thing is a bat flying.' This is scored W for location, animal movement (FM) as the determinant, A for content, good form quality, and a popular response. Option c is right because H, A and N name what the response depicts, which is its content. Exam trap: location codes (W, D, Dd, S) and determinant codes (F, M, C) are also letters, so the abbreviations blur together; ask whether a code says where, why or what.", "traps": {"a": "Location scores where on the blot the response was seen: W for the whole blot, D for a common detail, Dd for an unusual detail and S for white space; H, A and N do not describe position.", "b": "Determinants score which feature of the blot shaped the response, such as form (F), human movement (M), colour (C) or shading; H, A and N describe what was seen, not why.", "d": "Form quality scores how well the percept fits the actual shape of the blot (good F+ or poor F-), a sign of reality testing; it does not classify the subject matter of the response."}}, {"q": "Perceptual speed and accuracy is commonly emphasised in:", "concept": "The answer is b: perceptual speed and accuracy is the ability most emphasised in clerical aptitude tests. An aptitude is a person's potential to succeed in a field with training; aptitude tests look forward, achievement tests look back. Clerical work, such as filing, checking accounts, copying records and data entry, needs a person to notice small differences quickly and without mistakes. So clerical tests usually present long lists of pairs of numbers or names and ask the examinee to mark, under a strict time limit, whether each pair is the same or different. The Minnesota Clerical Test, with number-checking and name-checking parts, is the classic example, and the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947) include a Clerical Speed and Accuracy subtest. These are speed tests: easy items, strict time. Example: Riya, applying for a bank clerk post, sees '4597312 4597312' and '68210945 68219045' and must mark 'same' or 'different' for hundreds of such pairs in a few minutes; only quick, careful checking catches the swapped digits in the second pair. Option b is right because clerical aptitude is defined by fast, accurate perception of detail. Exam trap: perceptual speed is also one of the primary mental abilities of Louis Thurstone (1938), so a general or scholastic answer can tempt, but scholastic aptitude tests stress verbal and numerical reasoning, not checking speed.", "traps": {"a": "Scholastic aptitude is the capacity for academic learning, measured mainly by verbal and numerical reasoning (as in the SAT, or the verbal reasoning and numerical ability subtests of the DAT); it does not centre on checking speed.", "c": "Mechanical aptitude is understanding mechanical principles and spatial relations, as in the Bennett Mechanical Comprehension Test with its pictured gears, pulleys and levers; speed of checking detail is not its focus.", "d": "Vocational aptitude is a broad term for fitness for an occupation as a whole, usually assessed with a battery of tests; it does not name the one specific ability of perceptual speed and accuracy."}}, {"q": "Which type of counselling emphasises more on intellectual aspects than emotional aspects?", "concept": "The answer is a: directive counselling emphasises the intellectual aspects of a problem more than the emotional aspects. Directive (counsellor-centred) counselling was set out by Edmund G. Williamson (1939) in How to Counsel Students, the 'Minnesota point of view', building on the trait-factor tradition of Frank Parsons (1909). It assumes the client's difficulty comes mainly from a lack of information or poor reasoning, so the counsellor, as the expert, gathers data, reasons out the problem and advises. Its six steps are analysis (collecting data from tests, records and interviews), synthesis (organising the data into a picture of strengths and weaknesses), diagnosis (identifying the problem and its causes), prognosis (predicting the likely outcome), counselling (advising and helping the client adjust) and follow-up. Non-directive (client-centred) counselling, from Carl Rogers (1942) in Counseling and Psychotherapy, reverses the emphasis: feelings and emotional attitudes matter more than intellectual analysis, and the client leads while the counsellor offers empathy, acceptance and genuineness. Eclectic counselling, associated with Frederick Thorne (1950), mixes the two as each case requires. Example: Kabir cannot choose between science and commerce. A directive counsellor reviews his aptitude scores and marks, explains the prospects of each stream and recommends commerce, appealing to reason. Option a is right because directive counselling relies on rational analysis and expert advice. Exam trap: directive and non-directive are easily reversed; directive means intellect and a counsellor who leads, non-directive means feelings and a client who leads. Group counselling is a format (several clients at once), not a style defined by intellect or emotion, so it cannot be the answer.", "traps": {"b": "Non-directive counselling (Carl Rogers, 1942) does the opposite: it puts feelings and emotional attitudes ahead of intellectual analysis and lets the client lead.", "c": "Group counselling is a format in which a counsellor works with several clients together; it can be run in a directive or non-directive way, so it does not by itself put intellect over emotion.", "d": "Eclectic counselling (Frederick Thorne, 1950) blends directive and non-directive methods as the case requires, so it gives no fixed priority to intellectual over emotional aspects."}}, {"q": "In the counselling situation the 'counsellee' is he:", "concept": "The answer is a: the counsellee is the person who undergoes counselling, the one who receives help. Counselling is a face-to-face helping relationship between two roles. The counsellor is the trained professional who conducts the sessions: builds rapport, listens, interviews, assesses and helps. The counsellee, usually called the client today, is the person who comes with a problem of living, such as a study difficulty, a career choice, a family conflict or worry, and works on it with the counsellor's help. The '-ee' ending marks the one who receives an action, as in employee or trainee, while '-or' marks the one who does it, as in employer or trainer. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, preferred the word 'client' to 'patient', because it suggests an active, responsible person who seeks help voluntarily rather than a sick person who is treated. The counsellee is usually a basically normal person with everyday problems of adjustment. Example: Meera, a Class 12 student anxious about her board examinations, meets the school counsellor every week. Meera is the counsellee; the school counsellor, who conducts the interviews and guides the sessions, is the counsellor. Option a is right because the counsellee is the one who undergoes, or receives, counselling. Exam trap: 'undertakes' and 'undergoes' look almost alike; to undertake counselling is to take on the job of giving it, which describes the counsellor.", "traps": {"b": "To undertake counselling means to take on the task of providing it, which describes the counsellor, the professional who gives the help, not the counsellee who receives it.", "c": "Conducting the interview is the counsellor's job; the interview is the counsellor's main tool, while the counsellee is the person being interviewed.", "d": "Taking care of a client describes a caregiver, or the counsellor's helping role; the counsellee is the client himself, the person who is helped."}}, {"q": "In group counselling the role of counsellor is as:", "concept": "The answer is a: in group counselling the counsellor acts as the group leader. Group counselling brings together a small group, usually about 6 to 10 members with similar concerns such as exam stress, homesickness or grief, to work on them with one or two trained counsellors. Members learn that others share their problems, get peer feedback and practise new behaviour safely. Irvin Yalom (1970) described these benefits as curative (now called therapeutic) factors, including universality, altruism, instillation of hope and interpersonal learning. Gerald Corey (1981), in Theory and Practice of Group Counseling, treats the counsellor in this setting as the group leader, who screens and prepares members, sets ground rules (such as confidentiality), facilitates interaction, links members' concerns, blocks harmful behaviour and models openness. A good group leader leads democratically: he guides the process but does not dictate what members say or decide. Example: Arjun, a college counsellor, runs an eight-member group for first-year hostel students who feel homesick. He sets the rules, invites quiet members to speak, links Riya's loneliness to similar feelings voiced by two others and sums up at the end. As leader he keeps the group safe and moving, while the members help each other. Option a is right because 'leader' is the standard name for the counsellor's role in a group. Exam trap: 'director' sounds close, but it implies authoritarian control, which group counselling avoids. Key note: some guidance texts describe the group counsellor as a helper or facilitator; a facilitating leader is still a leader, and leader is the standard role name in group counselling, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Helper describes every counsellor in any setting, individual or group, so it does not name the specific role the counsellor takes in a group.", "c": "Director implies authoritarian control over what members say and decide; group counselling aims at democratic, facilitative leadership in which members help each other.", "d": "Follower would mean giving up the role; the group needs someone to form it, set the rules, protect members and guide the process, which is the leader's job."}}, {"q": "The first step in the counselling process is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first step in the counselling process is building rapport, a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor builds it by greeting the client warmly, giving full attention (eye contact, an open posture, nodding), listening without judging, reflecting feelings, explaining what counselling involves and promising confidentiality within its limits. Carl Rogers (1957) listed six necessary and sufficient conditions for change, starting with psychological contact between counsellor and client; the counsellor's part is empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness. Gerard Egan (1975), in The Skilled Helper, likewise opens the helping process with attending to the client and helping him explore his concerns. Indian guidance texts give the process as building rapport, exploring the problem, assessment (including tests where needed), setting goals, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without trust, clients hide or soften the real problem, so later steps rest on half the facts. Example: Farah, a Class 9 student, is sent to the school counsellor for missing classes. Asked at once 'Why are you bunking?', Farah says nothing. If the counsellor first chats about Farah's favourite subject, listens patiently and promises confidentiality, Farah soon admits that she is being teased on the school bus. Option c is right because exploring, testing and problem solving all depend on the trust built at the start. Exam trap: discussing the problem feels like the obvious opening, since the client came with a problem, but real discussion begins only once rapport exists; and solving the problem is not a step the counsellor performs for the client.", "traps": {"a": "Administering tools such as aptitude or personality tests belongs to the assessment stage, which comes after rapport; tests given to a tense, distrustful client yield guarded, unreliable answers.", "b": "Discussing and exploring the problem is the second stage; a client will not open up about painful matters until rapport and trust have been built.", "d": "Solving the client's problem is not a step the counsellor carries out; counselling helps the client reach his own solution, and any resolution comes near the end of the process, not at the start."}}, {"q": "Counselling services are required:", "concept": "The answer is d: counselling services are needed throughout life, not at one school level only. Guidance and counselling are described as a continuous, lifelong process, because every stage of life brings its own choices and problems of adjustment. At the elementary level children need help settling into school, building study habits and coping with learning difficulties, fears or trouble at home. At the middle level, early adolescence brings peer pressure and questions of identity. At the secondary level students choose subjects and streams, prepare for board examinations and begin to think about careers. Adults face jobs, marriage, illness, loss and retirement. Developmental psychology supports this view: Erik Erikson (1950) described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age, each with its own crisis, and Donald Super (1957) treated career development as a life-span process running from growth and exploration through establishment and maintenance to decline (later called disengagement). Example: Kabir sees a school counsellor in Class 3 for reading problems, a career counsellor in Class 11 to choose between science and commerce, a workplace counsellor at 35 for job stress and a counsellor again at 62 when he struggles to adjust to retirement. Option d is right because the need for counselling arises at every stage, from childhood to old age. Exam trap: because Indian texts discuss guidance mainly within schools, the secondary level, where subject and career choices are made, looks like the answer; but a basic principle of guidance is continuity, so it is needed throughout the life span.", "traps": {"a": "The elementary level does need counselling for adjustment, study habits and learning problems, but choosing it alone ignores the needs of adolescents, adults and the elderly.", "b": "The secondary level is where subject and career choices make counselling especially visible, but it is only one stage of a lifelong need.", "c": "The middle level, with early adolescence and peer pressure, also needs counselling, but limiting services to it leaves out every other stage of life."}}, {"q": "Guidance is:", "concept": "The answer is b: guidance is the promotion of the use of one's own potential, helping a person discover and develop his abilities and make his own choices. Guidance is a process of helping individuals understand themselves (their abilities, interests and limits) and their opportunities, so that they can make wise educational, vocational and personal decisions and direct their own lives. Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (1951), in An Introduction to Guidance, stress that guidance is not giving directions or imposing one's views: it helps a person of any age manage his own life, develop his own points of view, make his own decisions and carry his own burdens. Other classic definitions agree: guidance helps each person, through his own efforts, to discover and develop his potentialities. So guidance is for everyone, is continuous, respects the right to choose and aims at self-direction. Example: Meera scores high on a mechanical aptitude test and enjoys repairing gadgets, but has never thought of engineering. Her guidance teacher shows her the results, discusses courses and careers with her and leaves the decision to her. Meera can now use a potential she had not recognised. Option b is right because guidance aims at the fullest use of a person's own abilities. Exam trap: 'All the above' tempts because guidance often does improve relationships, but guidance does not try to change anyone's personality; it works with the person as he is. Key note: guidance can improve relationships too, but 'all the above' fails because guidance does not set out to change personality, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Improving the quality of relationships can be a by-product of guidance, and it is a goal of family or marital counselling, but it is not the definition of guidance, which centres on the individual's own development and choices.", "c": "Guidance does not aim to change a person's personality; it helps the individual understand and use the abilities he already has, and deep personality change belongs, if anywhere, to psychotherapy.", "d": "All the above fails because option c is false: guidance respects the individual as he is and does not set out to change his personality."}}, {"q": "Who is known as father of guidance movement in American education?", "concept": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908) is known as the father of the guidance movement in American education. Parsons, a Boston engineer, lawyer and social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people, many of them immigrants, choose work wisely instead of drifting into whatever jobs they found. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, a year after his death. In it he set out three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, interests, limitations); knowledge of the requirements, advantages and prospects of different kinds of work; and 'true reasoning' on the relations between these two groups of facts. This matching of person and job became the trait-factor approach, later systematised by Edmund G. Williamson (1939) as directive counselling. Example: following Parsons, a counsellor helping Arjun would first assess his abilities and interests, then gather facts about careers such as nursing, accountancy and electrical work, and finally reason out with him which job fits him best. Option b is right because Parsons founded organised vocational guidance and is the figure standard texts call the father of the guidance movement. Exam trap: Jesse B. Davis (Vocational and Moral Guidance, 1914) brought guidance lessons into American classrooms, so he is a tempting 'education' answer, and Edmund G. Williamson is famous for directive counselling, a later development. Key note: Jesse B. Davis built guidance into the school curriculum first, and some American texts honour him as the first school counsellor, but Frank Parsons is the figure standard texts call the father of the guidance movement, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Edmund G. Williamson (1939) developed directive, counsellor-centred counselling (the Minnesota point of view) from Parsons's trait-factor ideas; he came three decades after the guidance movement began.", "c": "Jesse B. Davis (Vocational and Moral Guidance, 1914), as a principal in Grand Rapids, Michigan, built weekly guidance lessons into English classes and is often called the first school counsellor, but the title of father of the guidance movement goes to Parsons.", "d": "John M. Brewer (Education as Guidance, 1932) of Harvard argued that all education is guidance and wrote early histories of the vocational guidance movement, but he followed Parsons rather than founding the movement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not the ethical principle of counselling?", "concept": "The answer is d: obligation is not one of the ethical principles of counselling. Karen Kitchener (1984), writing in The Counseling Psychologist, proposed five moral principles as the foundation for ethical decisions in counselling. Respect for autonomy means honouring the client's right to make his own choices, as long as they do not harm others; it underlies informed consent and confidentiality. Beneficence means doing good: acting for the client's welfare and offering competent help. Nonmaleficence means doing no harm, including avoiding harmful techniques and exploitative dual relationships. Justice means fairness: treating clients equally and giving fair access to services. Fidelity means keeping promises and being trustworthy. Kitchener added fidelity to the four biomedical principles of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979); the American Counseling Association's code later added veracity (truthfulness). Example: Riya, a counsellor, learns that her client Arjun wants to leave engineering for music. Respecting his autonomy, she helps him weigh the choice but does not decide for him; she keeps his disclosures private as promised (fidelity), refers him on if his low mood worsens beyond her competence (beneficence and nonmaleficence), and gives him the same time and care as her other clients (justice). Option d is right because 'obligation' names none of the principles in these lists. Exam trap: counsellors certainly have obligations, and duties run through every code, so 'obligation' sounds ethical; but it is a general word for duty, not a named principle.", "traps": {"a": "Respect for autonomy, the client's right to make his own decisions, is one of Karen Kitchener's five principles and underlies informed consent, so it is an ethical principle of counselling.", "b": "Beneficence, the duty to do good and promote the client's welfare, is a core principle in both Karen Kitchener's list and the biomedical list of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress.", "c": "Nonmaleficence, 'above all, do no harm', is also a core principle; it differs from beneficence because it forbids causing harm rather than requiring the counsellor to do good."}}]});
